1 NAME Peter /Wirzske Halv Lilja/
2 GIVN Peter
2 SURN Wirzske Halv Lilja
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 PLAC Småland, Sweden
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1270
2 PLAC Njudung, Jönköping, Småland, Sweden
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Peter Wirzske=
2 CONT
2 CONT *Married: Ingrid Abjörnsdotter Sparre
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=++Halsten+Petersson&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=31&postid=sdhk_14977&tab=post&FacettState=undefined%3Ac%7C#tab '''SDHK no: 14977''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1398 July 26'''
2 CONT * '''Place of issue: Nydala monastery'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Content
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Halsten Petersson transfers to Nydala monastery the farm in "Lanxrum" in Svenarum parish that his late father Peter "Wirzske" previously donated to the monastery.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000037701436443&photo_id=6000000191076416828&position=0 '''Original:  Or. perg. RA 0101  images''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter was born about 1270.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is not much to find about Peter: But Peter in Njudung in Småland (in Sweden) probably carried the same half lily in Ginbalk, (on the cross), as his son Halsten Peterson. (Jan Raneke writes, a half Lily on Delinga, which is w
2 CONC rong).
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter in Njudung was probably born around the 1270s - he was married to Ingrid Abjørnsdotter Sparre (The Abjørnske Sparre clan in Flundre district). She was also married to the Norwegian Håkan Bårdson. (it says he was her first hu
2 CONC sband, but he is still mentioned in 1341, so he must have been her second husband, because Ingrid's son Halsten Petersson was mentioned already in 1329 - and died in 1358. There is a letter dated 21.Sept.1340 that points out Ingri
2 CONC d Abjørnsdotter as the mother of Halsten Peterson (Halv Lilja). A man named Olof Gren (Ant.Olof Ebbesson Sparre), according to the Vestergøtalagen to Halsten Peterson upplåta alle de gods in Fråstad that Håkan Bårdson's wife Ingri
2 CONC d Abjørnsdotter had inherited from her aunt (Tante) Bengta Madsdotter. This indicates that it is Halsten Peterson who takes over the inheritance from his mother. (Bengta Madsdotter, was the sister of Lucia Madsdotter, died no earl
2 CONC ier than 1338 when she made her will, (mother of Ingrid Abjørnsdotter). Halsten Peterson Halv Lilja, had a (half-brother? Arvid Peterson who himself carried a Sparre in the Våpnet, if he was a half-brother, Peter must have been ma
2 CONC rried twice! A Sigrid Petersdatter (Sparre) is mentioned, and was married to Nils Gusa DÄ (Snedbjälke), and is said to probably belong to the same branch as the county chief Jöns Petersson (Sparre) at Eknaholm and sister to Arvid 
2 CONC Petersson (Sparre). Sigrid's granddaughter Nils Nilsson Guse became the owner of Eknaholm through inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Bo J. Theutenberg writes in his book "Doktor Johannes Copp von Raumenthal, ett livsöde i reformationens spår" published in 2003=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Bo J. Theutenberg writes in his book "Doktor Johannes Copp von Raumenthal, ett livsöde i reformationens spår" published in 2003 which is part of the Skara Stiftshistoriska sälläpä writing series "no. 14" pp. 150 and 151 ab
2 CONC out Hallsten Petersson and his origin. There are several documents in which the children of Abjörn (Sparre) and Lucia Matsdotter (the older bulls) leave their farms to their blood relative Hallsten Petersson, who bore the coat of 
2 CONC arms a split yellow lily in a red field. The 1329 – 1357 (or 1358) mainly in Småland Njudung knight Hallsten Petersson, King Magnus Eriksson's fiduciary, shows in his coat of arms a briseur of lily - the coat of arms, a change of 
2 CONC an original lily - coat of arms. This points to the existence of an old lily-bearing family in Njudung, which has had contact with a lily-bearing family in the area around Halleberg and Hundeberg along the Göta River. It is probab
2 CONC ly one and the same lily - family, where the different members brandish their lily - weapons in different ways. The farms in Fors and Åsbräcka parishes in Flundre district were only a few miles from the church in Gärdhem, where th
2 CONC e lily stones are found, and to which Mrs. Lucia's sister Bengta Matsdotter donated gifts in her will in 1338, probably because there were old family ties between the farms and the families in Gärdhem and the farms and families in
2 CONC  Flundre district. The lily-bearer Hallsten Petersson was thus blood-stained with the Abjörnska Sparreätten in Flundre Härad. The close ties are particularly shown by the fact that Hallsten Petersson's brother Arvid Petersson hims
2 CONC elf carried Sparre in his coat of arms. In the work itself, it can be stated that the lily-bearer Hallsten Petersson was the granddaughter of the knight Abjörn (Sparre) and wife Lucia Matsdotter, as was probably the brother Arvid 
2 CONC Petersson (Sparre). This is proven by the fact that Abjörn and Lucia's daughter Margareta Abjörnsdotter, when she sells her share in the farm Berg in Fråstad in 1341 (DS 3591), mentions Hallsten Petersson as her blood relative ( d
2 CONC iscreto viro consanguineo meo Halstano Pætherson) it is further proven by Margareta's will from 1342 (DS 3614), in which she directly mentions him as her sororio, i.e. as her nephew (item sororio meo Halstano duas marchas et tantu
2 CONC m fratri suo Aberno). Thus Hallsten Petersson, like probably his brother Abjörn (Aberno), possibly a misspelling for the above-mentioned brother Arvid, belonged to the Abjörnska Sparreätten in Flundre district on the mother's side
2 CONC . The one of the sisters who is Hallsten Petersson's mother is most likely Ingrid Abjörnsdotter, who in addition to her marriage to the Norwegian Håkan Bårdsson must have been married to Hallsten Petersson's father, Peter NN from 
2 CONC Njudung, who may be assumed to have carried the same coat of arms as the son Hallsten . Here a connection appears between the lily family and one of the old Abjörn Sparreättar, which group consisted of the Sparre family of Tofta, 
2 CONC of the Abjörn Sparreätten in Flundra county and of Abjørn Ulfsson's Sparreätt from Finnveden. It is especially a letter dated 21 September 1340 (DS 3520) that points out Ingrid Abörnsdotter (Sparre) as the mother of the knight Hal
2 CONC lsten Petersson. In this, a man named Olof Gren commits himself to, according to the Västgötalagen, assign to Hallsten Petersson all the estates in Fråstad that Håkan Bårdsson's wife Ingrid Abjörnsdotter had inherited from her aun
2 CONC t Bengta Matsdotter. This indicates that it is Hallsten Petersson who takes over the inheritance from his mother. The mentioned Olof Gren is probably identical to the Olof Ebbesson (Sparre) who appears in a letter dated in Uplanda
2 CONC  near Kinnekulle on April 9, 1367 (RBP no. 761) and who, according to a letter on October 4, 1366 (DS 7428), was brother to Enegisle Ebbesson (Sparre), who on 29 June 1364 (DS 7020) appears as district chief in Rädväg's district. 
2 CONC The father of the brothers Olof Ebbesson and Erengisle Ebbesson, Ebbe Gren (Sparre) acts closely to the Sparre - family known as the Unnesönnernas Sparre family, which in turn is close to Jöns Abjörnsson's Sparre over the sjøblads
2 CONC  - family (so the Forstäna family).
2 CONT
2 CONT King Magnus of Sweden grants Hallsten Petersson Gäddegöl, Svarthöfdaryd, Ulvsmåla, Eringsboda and Broddamåla. 25 April 1338. Source: http://www.diplomatarium.dk/dd/supplement/1338-04-25.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallsten Petersson's family tree: http://www.roskildehistorie.dk
2 CONT
