1 OCCU Ordensriddare
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Johan Elofsson (Elofssönerna)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Elof, stamfar Elofssönerna
2 CONT * Mentioned 1281-1295. Mentioned no later than 11-11-1295. Knight of the Order in 1281 he was a knight and a member of the German Order. In 1281 he and his brother Anders gave to his sister Ingrid and her foundation the estates in
2 CONC  Nyckelby in Västra Stenby sn and Möhammar (now Medhamra) in Hagebyhöga sn all in Aska hd in Östergötland and also estates in Gräsgårds hd on Öland. Confirmed in 1285 a much earlier donation to Nydala monastery by Melby in Segerst
2 CONC ads sn in Gräsgård's hd as a soul gift for himself and his deceased wife. Exchanged 1285 purchase goods in Gräsgårds hd for Nydala monastery goods on Selaön in Selebo hd in Södermanland. Approved as a representative and guardian o
2 CONC f the brother Anders' children for his "nepotes" inheritance distribution after his daughter 4/11 1295, according to which he as a brother of the Order waived the inheritance in favor of his nephews. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?ValdSortering=DatumStigande&PageSize=20&Sokord=Kristina&EndastDigitaliserat=False&FacettLimits=IfjiTQ%3A0&AvanceradSok=True&page=35&FacettState=undefined%3Ac%7C&postid=sdhk_1678&tab=post#tab '''SDHK 
2 CONC No:1678''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/dokument/sdhk/1678.pdf '''Dating: 1295 November 4 Place:  of issue Sigtuna''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Johan Elofsson,''' Johan Ingevaldsson on behalf of Folke Jonsson and this Folke himself, Filip (Finvidsson) of Rumby' on behalf of his children, Magnus Gregersson on behalf of his wife and Inge Bursvenannounces that at the fune
2 CONC ral of Mrs. Kristina, Johan Elofsson's daughter, as guardian of the said Kristina's heirs approved and confirmed her testamentary appointment concerning the farms Edö and Öna with Åmme mill and meadows in Hammar (in Närke), which 
2 CONC she handed over to her husband Birger Petersson for the redemption of the testamentary gifts in said will, and further ordered and confirmed that Kristina's heirs shall not claim Mrs Kristina's legal inheritance from Mrs Birger's 
2 CONC property or bother him or his heirs regarding it. Birger Petersson, for his part, declares the wife's heirs free from all claims on the inheritance from the wife's property that would legally accrue to him. The witnesses were Domp
2 CONC rost Andreas in Uppsala, Mr. Röd and his brother Mr. Orestes (!), Mr. Karl Erlandsson, Mr. Johan Ängel, senior lecturer Israel in Sigtuna, lawyer Håkan, Eskil "skielghi", Karl "høcher", Olof in Ekeby, Johannes "gasabogh", Johannes
2 CONC  "chobucher", Olof from "languthoru" and Sixten. Sealants are the issuers, Provost Andreas and Messrs. Röd, Orestes and Karl Erlandsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''SEAL''' 8 of 11 preserved: 1, 4, 10, 11 spiritual, damaged; 7, 8, 9 dropped out; 2, 3, 5, 6, less injured. Nr 1: domprost Andreas Andersson Ands (Sv. Sig. II: 93, 95), nr 2: Johan Ingevaldssons (Sv. Sig. III: 91), nr 3: Filip
2 CONC  Finvidssons (Sv. Sig. III: 97), nr 4: kanik Karl Erlandssons (Sv. Sig. II: 123), nr 5: Magnus Gregerssons (Sv. Sig. II: 98), nr 6: Röd Keldorssons (Sv. Sig. III: 99), nr 10: kanik Karl Erlandssons (? Cf. no. 4, above; Sv. Sig. II
2 CONC : 123), nr 11: lektor Israels i Sigtuna (Sv. Sig. II: 114).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=16214 Johan Elofsson, by Swedish Biographical Dictionary, Retrieved 2023-01-28]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Born:1200'''
2 CONT * '''Death:1300 Knights of the Order'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Volume 13 (1950), page 409.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Merits'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Johan Elofsson (Johannes Elaui, Elefson, Elepssun). Called himself from 1285 brother of the knighthood of Christ in Livonia (frater milicie Christi de Liuonia), i.e. the then German order; still alive 4 Nov. 1295. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Was gm a daughter (d. before 1281) of Karl Tjälvesson of Fånö (the Fånö family), sister of the knights Johan Karlsson and Peter Karlsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The prerequisites for J.E's operations include a. the advance of the Dominicans into Northern Europe and the crusade activities of the Baltic Knights. In 1219, brother Simon from Sweden in Bologna had been accepted into the Domini
2 CONC can order as the first Swede there. Then the order went on a triumphal march all over the Nordic countries, got a monastery in Lund in 1223, was in Visby in 1230 and got its first two convents on the Swedish mainland in Sigtuna an
2 CONC d Skänninge in 1237, so that before the middle of the century it also gained a foothold in Skara, Lödöse and Kalmar. At the same time, at the request of the Teutonic Order in Prussia, a series of letters were dispatched from the P
2 CONC apal Curia in the years 1230–63 to the Dominicans in various provinces, including Dacia (the North), with calls for crusades against the infidel Prussians and Livers. These bulls made the Baltic crusade preaching almost a privileg
2 CONC e for the Dominicans. In Riga, in 1202, a different military order than the German one had been founded, namely the Brothers of the Knighthood of Christ (Fratres militiae Christi), confirmed in 1204. After their costume (white man
2 CONC tle with downward facing sword on the left side) they were usually called brothers of the sword, and the highest class, the knight brothers, were called sword knights. Although this order was included in the Teutonic Order in 1237
2 CONC , whose costume it then adopted, the former branch of sword brothers retained a certain independence in the country of origin and was then called the Teutonic Order in Livonia (Ordo Theutonicorum in Livonia). The papal call to par
2 CONC ticipate in the Order's struggle in Livonia was followed by at least two Swedes, partly and the highest class, the knight brothers, were called sword knights. Although this order was included in the Teutonic Order in 1237, whose c
2 CONC ostume it then adopted, the former branch of sword brothers retained a certain independence in the country of origin and was then called the Teutonic Order in Livonia (Ordo Theutonicorum in Livonia). The papal call to participate 
2 CONC in the Order's struggle in Livonia was followed by at least two Swedes, partly and the highest class, the knight brothers, were called sword knights. Although this order was included in the Teutonic Order in 1237, whose costume it
2 CONC  then adopted, the former branch of sword brothers retained a certain independence in the country of origin and was then called the Teutonic Order in Livonia (Ordo Theutonicorum in Livonia). The papal call to participate in the Or
2 CONC der's struggle in Livonia was followed by at least two Swedes, partlyKarl Ulfsson(Erikskrönikan's junker Karl, son of earl Ulf Fase), drowned at Durbin in 1260, partly JE, but nothing more is known about his activities in Livland.
2 CONC  There is an example from the time of Magnus Ladulås of a great man who made a promise to undertake a crusade there, but was unable to realize the idea, which is why in his will (April 23, 1286) he instead paid out a sum of money 
2 CONC as compensation for the promise (ad redemptionem crucis in Rigam), but since JE is directly referred to as a knight, he should have been actively active. Despite the merger with the Teutonic Order, however, brother JE always kept 
2 CONC the original order designation (frater milicie Christi de Liuonia or de ordine militum Christi) and is therefore usually referred to in the literature as a knight of the sword, which, however, is not entirely correct, since it was
2 CONC  the then Teutonic Order he belonged to.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family-inherited piety of J. E. and his circle of siblings is what left the most traces in the records. The first letter in which JE appears is his letter of gift in 1285 of an Öland farm to the Cistercian monastery in Nydala.
2 CONC  However, it was above all the Dominicans who won his heart, and this was also connected with his sister Saint Ingrid's activities for the founding of a nunnery at St. Martin's church in Skänninge, where both brothers, Johan and A
2 CONC nders (cf. genealogy .), gift of land (odat. letter). At the same time, all the siblings had a connection with "our first author", the famous Dominican monk Petrus de Dacia(H. Schück), and thereby brought into his world of mystery
2 CONC , asceticism and ecstasy, exemplified above all in the one whom his soul loved, the young Kristina in the village of Stommeln near Cologne. The biography of St. Christina, Petri's letter to her and Christina's reply and letters fr
2 CONC om their friends are the precious literary legacy left by the strange Dominican friar. Among these letters is also one from brother JE Already when Petrus arrived in Skänninge from Germany in the spring of 1271, he had contact wit
2 CONC h the family, first probably with the sisters Ingrid and Kristina, who then entered or entered the order. It is, among other things, a. about their establishment of the nunnery, which JE tells in the letter that brother Petrus wri
2 CONC tes down in 1281 (the year according to Gallén) to the presumably illiterate knight. "From my ancestors, I have inherited a special fondness for the order of the preacher brothers, and this fondness is shared by my whole family", 
2 CONC brother Johan assures Kristina av Stommeln there. He mentions the convention plans in Skänninge and can tell that the king has now donated a beautiful and suitable place there for a female Dominican monastery. From their paternal 
2 CONC inheritance, JE, his brother Anders and his two sisters, of whom Kristina is now deceased, have donated to the monastery. The letter continues: »I am telling you this because I want to invite you there and because I wish that you 
2 CONC may fill the place of my dead sister Kristina, your namesake, in the monastery». Several wish this, it says. "May the almighty God grant me the comfort of meeting you personally in this life," concludes brother Johan. Petrus de Da
2 CONC cia added a postscript to the invitation insistently agreeing. Although Saint Christina of Stommeln never came herself, her brother Gerardus became a Dominican brother in Sweden under the Nordic name Sigwinus, first a novice in Vä
2 CONC sterås in 1282, then in Visby (lived in 1289). When King Magnus, probably in 1282, according to the Chronicle of Erik himself, laid »the first stone» to Skänninge Monastery, the Elofsson family had their great day of joy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The documents do not bear witness to any of J.E.'s political deeds, although he belonged to a family circle with extensive public interests. His wife's uncles were the well-known knights Johan and Birger Filipsson (see Aspenäsätte
2 CONC n in vol. 2, p. 394 ff.), and with the son-in-law, the notorious lawman Birger Petersson(Finstaätten), who in a later marriage had the daughter St. Birgitta, JE has been in close contact. When Mr. Birger's first wife Kristina Joha
2 CONC nsdotter made her magnificent will in July 1293 with donations to a long list of church purposes, monasteries and hospitals, JE was apparently called upon. To the father and his orders, wife Kristina gave her farm Sörby in Aska di
2 CONC strict (Ög.) together with half an attung in Varvs sn in the same district and 100 marks in money. In a special letter of confirmation, dated Broby in Strå sn (Ög.), JE says that although both he and his order according to papal p
2 CONC rivilege can enter as heirs in the same way as laymen, he has, out of concern for the spiritual welfare of his dead daughter and to comfort for other heirs after him, only retaining the gifts specified in the will. No other childr
2 CONC en of JE than Kristina are known. The literature data, that either a J.E's sister (Kjellberg) would have been the gm of the Uppland magnate Filip Finvidsson to Rumby (Rundby), or that this would have been a niece (Ståhle) to Johan
2 CONC  and Birger Filipssöner due to a misunderstanding; this was J.E's brother-in-law in that he was married to another daughter of Karl Tjälvesson of Fånö (and thus a niece of Johan and Birger Filipssöner), as Gallén and Liljeholm hav
2 CONC e shown with great clarity. In his last letter, dated in the Dominicans' Sigtuna 4 Nov. 1295, JE acts as guardian for the now deceased brother Anders' children and also for nieces, who in that case must have been after sister Kris
2 CONC tina (Liljeholm; it is less likely that they would have been sister Ingrid's, as Gallén 1949 doubtlessly questioned) . the Uppland magnate Filip Finvidsson to Rumby (Rundby), or that he would have been the nephew (Ståhle) of Johan
2 CONC  and Birger Filipssöner is due to a misunderstanding; this was J.E's brother-in-law in that he was married to another daughter of Karl Tjälvesson of Fånö (and thus a niece of Johan and Birger Filipssöner), as Gallén and Liljeholm 
2 CONC have shown with great clarity. In his last letter, dated in the Dominicans' Sigtuna 4 Nov. 1295, JE acts as guardian for the now deceased brother Anders' children and also for nieces, who in that case must have been after sister K
2 CONC ristina (Liljeholm; it is less likely that they would have been sister Ingrid's, as Gallén 1949 doubtlessly questioned) . the Uppland magnate Filip Finvidsson to Rumby (Rundby), or that he would have been the nephew (Ståhle) of Jo
2 CONC han and Birger Filipssöner is due to a misunderstanding; this was J.E's brother-in-law in that he was married to another daughter of Karl Tjälvesson of Fånö (and thus a niece of Johan and Birger Filipssöner), as Gallén and Liljeho
2 CONC lm have shown with great clarity. In his last letter, dated in the Dominicans' Sigtuna 4 Nov. 1295, JE acts as guardian for the now deceased brother Anders' children and also for nieces, who in that case must have been after siste
2 CONC r Kristina (Liljeholm; it is less likely that they would have been sister Ingrid's, as Gallén 1949 doubtlessly questioned) . or that this would have been the nephew (Ståhle) of Johan and Birger Filipssöner due to a misunderstandin
2 CONC g; this was J.E's brother-in-law in that he was married to another daughter of Karl Tjälvesson of Fånö (and thus a niece of Johan and Birger Filipssöner), as Gallén and Liljeholm have shown with great clarity. In his last letter, 
2 CONC dated in the Dominicans' Sigtuna 4 Nov. 1295, JE acts as guardian for the now deceased brother Anders' children and also for nieces, who in that case must have been after sister Kristina (Liljeholm; it is less likely that they wou
2 CONC ld have been sister Ingrid's, as Gallén 1949 doubtlessly questioned) . or that this would have been the nephew (Ståhle) of Johan and Birger Filipssöner due to a misunderstanding; this was J.E's brother-in-law in that he was marrie
2 CONC d to another daughter of Karl Tjälvesson of Fånö (and thus a niece of Johan and Birger Filipssöner), as Gallén and Liljeholm have shown with great clarity. In his last letter, dated in the Dominicans' Sigtuna 4 Nov. 1295, JE acts 
2 CONC as guardian for the now deceased brother Anders' children and also for nieces, who in that case must have been after sister Kristina (Liljeholm; it is less likely that they would have been sister Ingrid's, as Gallén 1949 doubtless
2 CONC ly questioned) . such as Gallén and Liljeholm with great clarity demonstrated. In his last letter, dated in the Dominicans' Sigtuna 4 Nov. 1295, JE acts as guardian for the now deceased brother Anders' children and also for nieces
2 CONC , who in that case must have been after sister Kristina (Liljeholm; it is less likely that they would have been sister Ingrid's, as Gallén 1949 doubtlessly questioned) . such as Gallén and Liljeholm with great clarity demonstrated
2 CONC . In his last letter, dated in the Dominicans' Sigtuna 4 Nov. 1295, JE acts as guardian for the now deceased brother Anders' children and also for nieces, who in that case must have been after sister Kristina (Liljeholm; it is les
2 CONC s likely that they would have been sister Ingrid's, as Gallén 1949 doubtlessly questioned) .
2 CONT
