2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Albert II, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg; c. 1268 – 22 September 1318), called the Fat (pinguis), was duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son of Albert the Tall, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Albert was a boy when his father died in 1279. He was first under guardianship of his uncle, Conrad, Bishop of Verden, and then of his elder brother, Henry I, Duke of 
2 CONC Brunswick-Lüneburg. In 1286 the three brothers divided their father's territory; Albert received the areas around Göttingen, Minden, Northeim, Calenberg, and Hanover. He made Göttingen his residence. In 1292, the third brother, Wi
2 CONC lliam, died childless, and Albert and Henry quarrelled about William's share, the areas around Brunswick and Wolfenbüttel; Albert finally prevailed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert married Rixa, daughter of Henry I, Prince of Werle, in 1284. They had the following children who reached adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide, married John, Landgrave of Lower Hesse 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richenza, Abbess of Gandersheim 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mechtild 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jutta 
2 CONT
2 CONT Luder, joined the Teutonic Order 
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II, Bishop of Halberstadt (died 1358) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Bishop of Hildesheim (died 1362) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto (died 1344) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus (died 1369) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernest (died 1367) 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT "Kövér"Albert herceg
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel
2 CONT
2 CONT joint reign with Henry I and William I
2 CONT
2 CONT 1279–1291
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert (Latin ''Albertus''; c. 1268 – 22 September 1318), called the Fat (''pinguis''), was duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son of Albert the Tall, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Albert was a boy when his father died in 1279. He was first under guardianship of his uncle, Conrad, Prince-Bishop of Verden, and then of his elder brother, Henry I, D
2 CONC uke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. In 1286 the three brothers divided their father's Principality of Wolfenbüttel; Albert received the areas around Göttingen, Minden, Northeim, Calenberg, and Hanover. He made Göttingen his residence, thus
2 CONC  Principality of Göttingen. In 1292, the third brother, William, died childless, and Albert and Henry, who had received the Principality of Grubenhagen, quarrelled about William's share, the remaining belittled areas around Brunsw
2 CONC ick and Wolfenbüttel; Albert finally prevailed.
2 CONT
