1 NOTE Eliška Richenza of Poland (Czech: Eliška Rejcka; Polish: Ryksa Elzbieta ) (1 September 1286 - 18 October 1335) was a daughter and the only surviving child of Przemysl II of Poland (originally prince of Greater Poland and Poznan)
2 CONC  and his second wife Richenza of Sweden, herself a daughter of Valdemar I of Sweden and Sofia of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her name at birth was Richenza; she adopted the name Eliška after her first marriage. She became queen consort of Bohemia as the second wife of Václav II and remained such as wife of Rudolf of Habsburg who succeeded Václav as K
2 CONC ing of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Her father, crowned as a King of Poland, was murdered on 10 February 1296 when she was a young child, and left no other heirs. Young Richenza, as the heiress / claimant of Poland, became an important participant in the dispute of 
2 CONC Polish throne. As a child she was engaged to the young Otto, son of the Margrave of Brandenburg and relative of her stepmother Margaret of Brandenburg. However, the boy died soon after.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage
2 CONT In 1300 Eliška Richenza (Rejcka) was married to a widower, King Václav II. of Bohemia (1271-1305, reigned in Bohemia from 1278 and in parts of Poland from 1304), who wanted to use her succession rights in order to gain Poland. H
2 CONC e had already subjugated Kraków in 1291, during the lifetime of Elisabeth's father. Because of her young age, the marriage was not celebrated until 26 May 1303, when she arrived in Prague and was crowned the Queen of Bohemia and 
2 CONC Poland, adopting the name Eliška at the same time (as Richenza was unused and seen as strange in Bohemia). Her husband Václav II died in Prague 21 June 1305 of tuberculosis. She acted as regent from August until October. Her ste
2 CONC pson Václav III (also a claimant to the throne of Hungary) succeeded the throne but was murdered in 1306 in Olomouc. The Kujavian branch of the Piast dynasty acquired Polish throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eliška Richenza had only one child, a daughter Ane?ka of Bohemia (15 June 1305 - c 1337), who was born just a few days before her husband's death. Ane?ka married a Silesian prince, Duke Henry I of Jawor, but remained childless. A
2 CONC ne?ka did have a pregnancy, but it was terminated in the first trimester, when she rode her horse over hill and dale causing the early termination, and kept her bed ridden for many months.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage
2 CONT Eliška was subsequently married 16 October 1306 to Rudolf I of Habsburg, son of Albert of Habsburg (King of Germany and Rome) who wanted to become king after her stepson. He was chosen Bohemia's king and Eliška remained queen. Y
2 CONC et only for a short time - Rudolf died 4 July 1307 of dysentery after becoming ill during the siege of the fortress of a nobleman in revolt. In his will Albert acknowledged Eliška's dowry towns and entailed her a huge amount of m
2 CONC oney.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Widowhood
2 CONT Queen Eliška's widowhood in Bohemia took place in quarreled times, as Emperors attempted to gain Bohemia, and the husbands of Elisabeth's stepdaughters were elevated to kingship, but their tenure was uncertain until John of Luxem
2 CONC burg, husband of Eliška's youngest stepdaughter (also named Eliška), succeeded in establishing his position.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her second husband's death Eliška left Prague and settled in Hradec Králové, one of her dowry towns, which became the center of her domain. She remained unmarried but lived with her lover, the powerful nobleman Jindrich o
2 CONC f Lipá (Henry of Lipá), who was the Moravian Hetman and Governor of Bohemian kingdom in the absence of king John of Luxemburg. They moved to Brno and spent there ten happy years (1318-1329) until his death. In this period she tu
2 CONC rned her attention to culture and religion, built churches and Cistercian convents (from 1323), and financed the making of illustrated hymn books. She died in Brno and was buried under the floor of her cloister church at the side 
2 CONC of her beloved Henry.
