1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1373
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==[https://saints.dh.gu.se/explore/people/1756 Ingegerd Karlsdotter (Stjärnbalk)]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Karl Dansson Stiernbielke and Ingeborg Magnusdotter oä Folkunge
2 CONT
2 CONT =Historical Biography & Proof Summary=
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingegärd Karlsdotter was a daughter of Karl Dansson (Stjärnbjälke) and Ingeborg Magnusdotter (of the illegitimate royal Folkunge/Bjälbo branch).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Historical Proof:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''SDHK-nr: 7289 (1358):''' Ingegärd is explicitly named alongside her sister Ingrid and mother Ingeborg in the inheritance split (arvskifte) following her father’s death.
2 CONT * '''SDHK-nr: 5837 & 7289:''' These primary sources prove she was married to Ragvald Nilsson (Fargalt), whom her father Karl Dansson explicitly calls his måg (son-in-law) as early as 1354. Through this marriage, the Fargalt family
2 CONC  inherited the prominent Hedensö estate.
2 CONT * '''SDHK-nr: 11853 (1381):''' She and her husband remained active in defending their inherited Södermanland properties in a major royal court case decades later.Genealogical 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Correction Note:=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Do not confuse her with her contemporary namesake who married Ragvald Erengislesson Puke. Older 19th-century noble registries incorrectly smashed those two lineages together because both women were named Ingegärd and both mar
2 CONC ried men named Ragvald. Modern Swedish medieval scholarship (Äldre svenska frälsesläkter) has separated them. Ingegärd Karlsdotter only married Ragvald Nilsson (Fargalt).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fargalt Fargalt, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fargalt is a modern name for a Swedish noble family that had a wild boar , or fargalt , in its coat of arms .
2 CONT
2 CONT The boar in the coat of arms is indicated in the painted coat of arms in Tövsala Church as black and yellow, and is indicated by the archaeologist Johan Peringskiöld as a black boar in a field of gold .
2 CONT
2 CONT The family was from Södermanland and came to belong to the High Friars. The oldest known member of the family is Ragvald Karlsson who sealed letters with his seal in 1334-1335. Judging from the seal, patronymic and place of reside
2 CONC nce, the squire Nils Ragvaldsson is his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils Ragvaldsson had property in Finspånga in Östergötland . He is mentioned in the sources from 1342 to 1349. He is apparently the Nils Ragvaldsson who, together with his sons Ragvald and Anders, was excommunicated in 1358 for as
2 CONC saulting a servant of the priest in Skällvik, Hammarkind district in Östergötland. He also had a third son named Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about Anders Nilsson and Magnus Nilsson. Anders is mentioned in 1356 and possibly 1370. Magnus is mentioned in 1354 and 1360.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragvald Nilsson was born around 1328. He was the district governor of Oppunda district in Södermanland in 1355 and again in 1356. He suffered a " stroke " on August 23, 1374 and became "crooked in the face and paralyzed in half of
2 CONC  his body". By throwing dice, it was decided that one should call upon the already then considered saint Birgitta Birgersdotter . Ragvald made a pilgrimage vow and the paralysis began to subside. He made a pilgrimage to Vadstena a
2 CONC nd became "completely healthy, rosy in the face and with a well-fed body". At the latest in 1354, he married Ingegärd Karlsdotter. She was the daughter of Karl Dansson (Stjärnbjälke) and Ingeborg Magnusdotter ( the illegitimate br
2 CONC anch of the Bjälbo family ).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragvald Nilsson had a daughter and two sons, the prebendary of Strängnäs Karl Ragvaldsson (mentioned 1382–1401) and the squire Magnus Ragvaldsson (mentioned 1399–1419). The latter owned Hedensö and Gräfseboda (now Haneberg in Eski
2 CONC lstuna ) and land in Södermanland. His sister Ingeborg Ragvaldsdotter was married to Magnus Elofsson (Tandlaätten) . [ 1 ] [ 2 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT The same family included the lawyer and councilor Erik Ragvaldsson (1463–1494), son of Ragvald Magnusson (Fargalt) (1426–1469) and Katarina Svensdotter ( Pik ). [ 3 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT With the councilor and lawman in the Södermanland law saga , Peder Ragvaldsson (Fargalt) , last mentioned in 1499, the family seems to have become extinct on the male side. He left behind two daughters who married two brothers of 
2 CONC the Kyle family , Margareta Pedersdotter was married to the squire Klas Kyle, and her sister Karin Pedersdotter, was married to her sister's brother-in-law (brother of Klas Kyle) the squire and lawman on Öland Johan Kyle .
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family Tree=
2 CONT
2 CONT 1- '''The squire'Nils Ragvaldsson,'''
2 CONT Son 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2em>2 -'''Ragvald Nilsson''' was born around 1328. He was the district chief in Oppunda district in Södermanland. Married Ingegärd Karlsdotter, daughter of Karl Dansson (Stjärnbjälke) and Ingeborg Magnusdotter ( illegitimate branc
2 CONC h of the Bjälbo family ).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3em>  Children
2 CONT 3em>1- '''Karl Ragvaldsson''' (mentioned 1382–1401).
2 CONT 3em>2- '''Ingeborg Ragvaldsdotter,''' married to Magnus Elofsson (Tandlaätten)
2 CONT 3em>3- '''squire Magnus Ragvaldsson''' (mentioned 1399–1419).
2 CONT 4em> Son
2 CONT 4em>1-'''Ragvald Magnusson (Fargalt)''' (1426–1469) married to Katarina Svensdotter ( Pik ).
2 CONT 5em>   Son
2 CONT 5em>1- '''Peder Ragvaldsson (Fargalt) ,''' last mentioned in 1499, with him the family seems to have become extinct on the male side.
2 CONT 7em> Children
2 CONT 7em>1 - '''Margareta Pedersdotter''' was married to the squire Klas Kyle .
2 CONT 7em>2 - '''Karin Pedersdotter,''' married to her sister's brother-in-law, the squire and lawman on Öland Johan Kyle (brother of Klas Kyle).
