1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ='''Johan Halstensson''' Halv lilja, till Dingelvik=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Johan Halstensson is listed in Halsten Petersson's Æt til Dingelvik, Stensby.
2 CONT * The Halsten Petersson family , also called the Halv Lilja , is a modern conventional name for a Swedish medieval noble family that took its name from the ancestor, the knight and bailiff Halsten Petersson. The family died out be
2 CONC fore the Swedish House of Knights was founded in 1625, which is why it was never introduced there as a noble family . The members of the family wore a red pieced shield, with half a golden lily laid beamwise in the upper field.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=Johan+Halstensson&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=63&postid=sdhk_45443&tab=post SDHK-nr: 45443]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''1410 without day*'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Brynolf and Lars Hansson transfer land in Halspånga to Johan Halstensson.''' Fasting in Österrekarne are Erik Petersson in Edeby, Lars Ersson, Lars in Hässelby, Lars in Torsberga, Herman in Skensta, Bengt in Måsta, Mattias in S
2 CONC trand, Mickel in Balsta, Abraham in Kjula, Olof(?) Östensson(?) in Hammarby and Ulf in Röksta. The wood tradesmen are Tomas Länsman and Nils Andersson. Sten Haraldsson's judge Nils Björnsson (see comment) seals the deal.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Post-medieval transcripts: (around 1600) Städer's files 65: Torshälla, Riksarkivet Literature and commentary Cf. SDHK no. 15493. Incomplete transcript, including regarding forms. The dating to around 1410 is based
2 CONC  on Sten Haraldsson's tenure and his use of Nils Björnsson (the transcript's "Nillss Brömss", not identical to Nils Bröms in Hedensö around the year 1500) as deputy; see J. E. Almquist, Law sagas and judgment sagas in Sweden (1954
2 CONC ), p. 116
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Johan Halstensson was born about 1390.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Peder Månsson Utter says in his genealogy about Halsten Petersson (c.1290/1300?) (Half Lilja) that his son Johan Halstensson was the father of Sven Jonsson (at Dingelvik), (Half Lilja). But the Johan who is mentioned as Halsten's 
2 CONC son must have been born around 1320? century, and in that case be a brother of Håkan Halstensen mentioned as of legal age already in 1346. Halsten Petersson (c.1290/1300) (Halv Lilja) died in 1358, and was married to Märta Knutsdo
2 CONC tter (Tre Rosor), and they can not have a son Johan who must have been born around 1390, and had a son Sven Jonsson born around 1420, it is unthinkable. So there must have been a Johan Halstensson born in the early 14th century wh
2 CONC o was the son of Halsten and Märta!
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Halstensson (ca. 1390? - ) had a son Sven Jonsson (Johansson) (Halv Lilja), born ca. 1420 - died ca. 1484, or a little earlier, and is mentioned at Herregården Dingelvik Forestena (Älvsborg län), married with Ingeborg Toresd
2 CONC otter Byting (born ca. 1425 - ).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1339 and 1340, an Agmund (Possibly Agmund Nilsson, Fjellagt Bjelke), mentioned in Tracksta (Vadsbro socken, Södermanland) as regular for Sigge Magnusson (SDHK-nr:4515), probably? the same as Sigge Magnusson to Lagmanö, (perhaps
2 CONC  born ca.1305?), father of Bengta Siggesdotter, and was thus father-in-law of Håkan Halstensson (Halv Lilja) (SDHK.nr:11725). Sigge is said to be married to a Ramborg, they also had a daughter Ingrid Siggesdatter who used Djurlö a
2 CONC s a homestead.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1344, Ramfrid is mentioned (SDHK no: 5039), this is probably ? Ramfrid Bengtsdotter (Lejon), who had his sister daughter Märta Knutsdotter (Vapen Tre Rosor) married to Halsten Petersson (Halv Lilja), (parents of Håkan Halstenss
2 CONC on), mentioned already in 1346 and must have been of legal age then, ant. born about 1320? the number. When Ramfrid died childless, the single will inherited 29 Sept. 1358 (Dals Dip.55) i.a. her sister's daughter Märta Knutsdotter
2 CONC , (she was then the widow of Halsten Petersson. (Halv Lilja)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1355, SOFI mentions a Halsten in Träcksta, and on 24 February 1358 a Halsten in Tracksta is found as a resident in Vadsbro (SDHK.nr:7300) From and including 18 May 1362 to and including 7 May 1401 we find Peter in Träcksta, men
2 CONC tioned a total of 8 times, mostly as fast- But in 1401 land Fyrby in Blacksta socken och in Vik Vadsbro sn, designated as Peter's right Byrdh. Peter in Träcksta does not appear anywhere with a patronymic icon, but it is close to a
2 CONC ssume that he was a son of the former Halsten Petersson, in which case Peter (Halstensson could have been born ca. 1330, to have been a son of Halsten Peterson and Marta Knutsdotter (Tre Rosor) and had a son Halsten Petersson (ca.
2 CONC  1360 - ). But in the sources Marta Knutsdotter (Tre Rosor) is mentioned only with her sons Håkan and Knut Halstensonner, who were still alive in 1385. (Kunne Halsten Petersson have been married before?), and Peder månsson
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halsten_Peterssons_%C3%A4tt Halsten Petersson's Family, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Halsten Petersson's family ,''' also called half lily , is a contemporary conventional name for a Swedish medieval squire family that took its name from the progenitor, knight and bailiff Halsten Petersson. The family died out 
2 CONC before Sweden's Riddarhus was founded in 1625, which is why it was never introduced there as nobility . The members of the family wore a red scotch, with half a golden lily laid in beams in the upper field.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''History'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Two land exchanges from 1377 and 1380 give good insight into the mutual kinship of several members.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Halsten Petersson, knight (1355), landowner and bailiff. His origin is unknown, but a (half) brother is mentioned, Arvid Petersson, who carried a spar in the coat of arms. 1336 and 1338 goods as thanks for faithful service. he was
2 CONC  given by King Magnus Eriksson but participated in King Erik Magnusson 's rebellion against his father, and is mentioned among Erik's five pledges in 1357, but is stated to have died on March 1, 1358. [ 3 ] Married to Märta (Marga
2 CONC reta) Knutsdotter ( Tre Rosor till Mörby ), daughter to Knut (Tre Rosor till Mörby) and Kristina Bengtsdotter in Haneström, daughter of the lawman in Västergötland Bengt Hafridsson . [ 4 ] [ 5 ]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Bo J. Theutenberg writes in his book "Doktor Johannes Copp von Raumenthal, ett livsöde i reformationens spår" published in 2003=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Bo J. Theutenberg writes in his book "Doktor Johannes Copp von Raumenthal, ett livsöde i reformationens spår" published in 2003 which is part of the Skara Stiftshistoriska sälläpä writing series "no. 14" pp. 150 and 151 ab
2 CONC out Hallsten Petersson and his origin. There are several documents in which the children of Abjörn (Sparre) and Lucia Matsdotter (the older bulls) leave their farms to their blood relative Hallsten Petersson, who bore the coat of 
2 CONC arms a split yellow lily in a red field. The 1329 – 1357 (or 1358) mainly in Småland Njudung knight Hallsten Petersson, King Magnus Eriksson's fiduciary, shows in his coat of arms a briseur of lily - the coat of arms, a change of 
2 CONC an original lily - coat of arms. This points to the existence of an old lily-bearing family in Njudung, which has had contact with a lily-bearing family in the area around Halleberg and Hundeberg along the Göta River. It is probab
2 CONC ly one and the same lily - family, where the different members brandish their lily - weapons in different ways. The farms in Fors and Åsbräcka parishes in Flundre district were only a few miles from the church in Gärdhem, where th
2 CONC e lily stones are found, and to which Mrs. Lucia's sister Bengta Matsdotter donated gifts in her will in 1338, probably because there were old family ties between the farms and the families in Gärdhem and the farms and families in
2 CONC  Flundre district. The lily-bearer Hallsten Petersson was thus blood-stained with the Abjörnska Sparreätten in Flundre Härad. The close ties are particularly shown by the fact that Hallsten Petersson's brother Arvid Petersson hims
2 CONC elf carried Sparre in his coat of arms. In the work itself, it can be stated that the lily-bearer Hallsten Petersson was the grandson of the knight Abjörn (Sparre) and wife Lucia Matsdotter, as was probably the brother Arvid Peter
2 CONC sson (Sparre). This is proven by the fact that Abjörn and Lucia's daughter Margareta Abjörnsdotter, when she sells her share in the farm Berg in Fråstad in 1341 (DS 3591), mentions Hallsten Petersson as her blood relative ( discre
2 CONC to viro consanguineo meo Halstano Pætherson) it is further proven by Margareta's will from 1342 (DS 3614), in which she directly mentions him as her sororio, i.e. as her nephew (item sororio meo Halstano duas marchas et tantum fra
2 CONC tri suo Aberno). Thus Hallsten Petersson, like probably his brother Abjörn (Aberno), possibly a misspelling for the above-mentioned brother Arvid, belonged to the Abjörnska Sparreätten in Flundre district on the mother's side. The
2 CONC  one of the sisters who is Hallsten Petersson's mother is most likely Ingrid Abjörnsdotter, who in addition to her marriage to the Norwegian Håkan Bårdsson must have been married to Hallsten Petersson's father, Peter NN from Njudu
2 CONC ng, who may be assumed to have carried the same coat of arms as the son Hallsten . Here a connection appears between the lily family and one of the old Abjörn Sparreättar, which group consisted of the Sparre family of Tofta, of th
2 CONC e Abjörn Sparreätten in Flundra county and of Abjørn Ulfsson's Sparreätt from Finnveden. It is especially a letter dated 21 September 1340 (DS 3520) that points out Ingrid Abörnsdotter (Sparre) as the mother of the knight Hallsten
2 CONC  Petersson. In this, a man named Olof Gren commits himself to, according to the Västgötalagen, assign to Hallsten Petersson all the estates in Fråstad that Håkan Bårdsson's wife Ingrid Abjörnsdotter had inherited from her aunt Ben
2 CONC gta Matsdotter. This indicates that it is Hallsten Petersson who takes over the inheritance from his mother. The mentioned Olof Gren is probably identical to the Olof Ebbesson (Sparre) who appears in a letter dated in Uplanda near
2 CONC  Kinnekulle on April 9, 1367 (RBP no. 761) and who, according to a letter on October 4, 1366 (DS 7428), was brother to Enegisle Ebbesson (Sparre), who on 29 June 1364 (DS 7020) appears as district chief in Rädväg's district. The f
2 CONC ather of the brothers Olof Ebbesson and Erengisle Ebbesson, Ebbe Gren (Sparre) acts closely to the Sparre - family known as the Unnesönnernas Sparre family, which in turn is close to Jöns Abjörnsson's Sparre over the sjøblads - fa
2 CONC mily (so the Forstäna family).
2 CONT
