1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sten_Sture_the_Elder '''Sten Sture the Elder'''] was a Swedish statesman and regent of Sweden 1470–1497 and 1501–1503. As the leader of the victorious Swedish separatist forces agains
2 CONC t the royal unionist forces during the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Brunkeberg Battle of Brunkeberg] in 1471, he weakened the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalmar_Union Kalmar Union] considerably and became the effect
2 CONC ive ruler of Sweden as Lord Regent for most of his remaining life.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Background===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In contemporary sources''' he is alternatively referred to as '''Sten Gustavsson''' or '''Herr Sten''' (''Lord Sten''); the practice of using noble family names as part of a personal name was not yet in widespread use in Sweden
2 CONC  at the time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was born''' around 1440, the son of Gustav Anundsson of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sture Sture] family and Birgitta Stensdotter [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bielke Bielke], half-sister of the future [https://en.wik
2 CONC ipedia.org/wiki/Charles_VIII_of_Sweden Charles VIII]. The Sture family was one of the high-ranking noble families of the time, though only distantly related to the royal house; his closest royal ancestor was [https://en.wikipedia.
2 CONC org/wiki/Sverker_II_of_Sweden King Sverker II] of Sweden (both through family of Vinga and through family of Aspenäs). Sture's father, Gustav Anundsson, was [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castellan Castellan] of [https://en.wikipe
2 CONC dia.org/wiki/Kalmar_Castle Kalmar Castle] and a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Privy_Council_of_Sweden Privy Councillor], but died when the son was four. Birgitta Stensdotter remarried Gustav Karlsson of the Gumsehuvud family, and
2 CONC  the son was most likely raised in their home, first at Kalmar Castle and later at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ekholmen_Castle Ekholmen Castle].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The 15th century''' in Sweden was largely defined by the political struggles and civil wars between the unionists of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalmar_Union Kalmar Union], seeking to unite Sweden with Denmark and Norway
2 CONC  under the rule of the Danish monarchs, with Danish support, and the separatists seeking to re-establish Sweden as an independent kingdom under a rival Swedish monarch. Due to his close family ties to the Swedish King Charles, the
2 CONC  young Sten Sture became part of the Swedish separatist political movement from an early age, and visited Charles during his exile in Danzig. He is mentioned as a knight in 1462 and as a privy councillor in 1466, and took up resid
2 CONC ence on the family estate at Räfsnäs north of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariefred Mariefred]. Sture fought with Bishop and Regent [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kettil_Karlsson_(Vasa Kettil Karlsson Vasa] during the uprising 
2 CONC against the Danish King [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_I_of_Denmark Christian I] in 1464, taking part in the decisive victory at Haraker. He served as a military commander under King Charles VIII, defeating Erik Karlsson
2 CONC  Vasa's uprising at Uppbo in 1470 and later in the same year successfully beating back Christian I's forces at Öresten.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He married''' [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ingeborg_Tott Ingeborg Tott], niece by marriage of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdalena_of_Sweden Magdalena of Sweden], in 1467; she was a renaissance personality interested in t
2 CONC heology and science and seems to have had some importance in the intellectual development during his reign. The marriage was childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===First regency===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sture's uncle, King''' Charles VIII named Sture heir to Charles' personal domains before his death in 1470, and left Sture in charge of the crown lands, including the city of Stockholm and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tre_Kro
2 CONC nor_(castle Stockholm Castle]. On the death of King Charles on 15 May 1470, Sture immediately became the most powerful noble and political force in the country and was elected Lord Protector and Regent of Sweden (riksföreståndare)
2 CONC  by the Riksmöte in Arboga on 1 May 1471. Sture consolidated his position through the victory of Brunkeberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''At the''' [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Brunkeberg Battle of Brunkeberg] on 10 October 1471, which was fought around Brunkebergsåsen outside the northern gates of Stockholm, his Swedish separatist army triumphed agai
2 CONC nst Danish King Christian I's Swedish unionist and Danish forces, injuring Christian and routing the unionist army. This victory elevated Sture to the position of a national savior. The sculpture [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sai
2 CONC nt_George_and_the_Dragon_(Notke Saint George and the Dragon] in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storkyrkan Storkyrkan] in Stockholm, commonly attributed to the German sculptor [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernt_Notke Bernt Notke]
2 CONC , was raised to commemorate the battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''For a quarter of''' a century he ruled Sweden making the regency almost an office in its own right. He was supported by the peasantry, the commercial interests of the mining district of Bergslagen and the lower nobility, playin
2 CONC g them out against the unionist high-ranked nobility and clergy and managing a difficult act of balance towards the Danish demands of reunion. In a meeting in Kalmar in 1483, the high nobility confirmed the new Danish king Hans as
2 CONC  the true king of Sweden on condition of extensive privileges and guarantees granted to the high nobility, clergy and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Privy_Council_of_Sweden Privy Council], but Sture nevertheless managed to hold on
2 CONC  to the political power for the time being, refusing to give up his office as Regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sture's reign saw''' the foundation of the first Swedish university, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uppsala_University Uppsala University], founded by Archbishop [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakob_Ulvsson Jakob Ulvsson] in 14
2 CONC 77 with support from Sture.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1493, the''' Danish and Norwegian king, Hans (also called John I), formed an alliance with [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_III_of_Russia Ivan III of Russia] against Sten Sture. From 1495 to 1497, Sten successfully repell
2 CONC ed a Russian invasion of Finland. However, he subsequently fell out with the majority of the Swedish nobility, most prominently [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Svante_Nilsson_(regent_of_Sweden Svante Nilsson], and the Swedish Privy
2 CONC  Council declared him to be deposed as regent on 8 March 1497.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Second regency===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In the ensuing war''', Sten was supported by peasant forces, but they were defeated at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Rotebro Battle of Rotebro] by [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John,_King_of_Denmark Hans of Denm
2 CONC ark] (also known as King John I of Denmark and Norway), who invaded Sweden in July the same year. On 6 October, Sten surrendered to King Hans in Stockholm and was reconciled with him. Hans was crowned King of Sweden, and Sten was 
2 CONC given the highest position of authority in Sweden below the King. However, during the next rebellion against the Danes in 1501, he again took office as regent, leading the Swedish struggle for independence until his death. In May 
2 CONC 1502, the unionist garrison in Stockholm, led by Queen [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_of_Saxony Christina], capitulated due to severe starvation after a drawn-out siege. In early 1503 his forces were in control of Sweden
2 CONC  apart from Kalmar and the island of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%96land Öland].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sture used the''' capture of the Queen for propaganda purposes, personally accompanying the Queen to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halmstad Halmstad] in Denmark after a cease-fire had been negotiated by the [https://en.wikiped
2 CONC ia.org/wiki/Hanseatic_League Hanseatic League], in October 1503.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death and aftermath===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''On his way back''' from Denmark, Sture fell ill and died on 14 December 1503. Bishop [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemming_Gadh Hemming Gadh], who accompanied Sture on the journey, alerted Svante Nilsson at [https://en.wikiped
2 CONC ia.org/wiki/Stegeborg_Castle Stegeborg Castle]. Sture's widow, Ingeborg Tott, who at the time was in control of the Stockholm garrison, was only presented with the news of her husband's death once Svante Nilsson had reached Stockh
2 CONC olm. She was persuaded to turn over Stockholm Castle and received [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A4me_Castle Häme Castle] in Finland as compensation.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sture had no''' legitimate children from his marriage with Ingeborg Tott, only a daughter born out of wedlock, Birgitta, who became a nun in Vadstena Abbey. The male line of the old Sture family, the Tre Sjöblad family, died ou
2 CONC t with Sten Sture. His closest relatives and heirs were his deceased sister's, the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Vasa house of Vasa], and his nephew [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erik_Johansson_Vasa Erik Johansson Vasa]
2 CONC , a knight and member of the Privy Council, inherited Sture's extensive personal domains in Södermanland and Uppland. Erik Johansson's son, the future [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustav_I_of_Sweden King Gustav I of Sweden] had 
2 CONC been born a few years before Sten's death. However, Erik Johansson was described by his contemporaries as a simpleminded and brutal man, prone to violent outbursts and on more than one occasion charged with manslaughter, and lacke
2 CONC d the political skill of his uncle Sten Sture.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Regent Sten was''' succeeded by his colleague from the Privy Council and former enemy Svante Nilsson, of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natt_och_Dag Natt och Dag] family, as regent. Svante Nilsson's son and successor, [http
2 CONC s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sten_Sture_the_Younger Sten Sture the Younger], later chose to adapt the Sture name for political purposes, despite only being distantly related.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Burial and monuments===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''To commemorate the''' victory at Brunkeberg, Sten Sture ordered a wooden monument for Storkyrkan in Stockholm, Saint George and the Dragon, built in the late 1480s. Saint George's horse carries the coat of arms of the Tre Sjöbl
2 CONC ad family, Sten Sture's coat of arms, and the monument is therefore interpreted as a symbol of his victory over Denmark, the dragon. A bronze copy of the monument was placed on Köpmantorget in Stockholm in 1912.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sten Sture was''' buried in the church of Mariefred Charterhouse, which he had helped establish and made significant contributions to during his reign. His remains were moved to Strängnäs Cathedral during the reign of Gustav I,
2 CONC  after the short-lived monastery was closed in 1526, during the protestant reformation. A memorial outside the town church of Mariefred marks the former site of the monastery. The present burial monument in the choir of [https://e
2 CONC n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Str%C3%A4ngn%C3%A4s_Cathedral Strängnäs Cathedral] was ordered in 1774 by [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustav_III_of_Sweden King Gustav III].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sture became a''' prominent symbolic figure in the 19th century nationalist movement in Sweden, together with the early 15th century rebel leader [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engelbrekt_Engelbrektsson Engelbrekt Engelbrektsso
2 CONC n] and King Gustav I who ended the Kalmar Union. A large stone monument to Sten Sture, by the sculptor [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Milles Carl Milles] (1875–1955), was inaugurated in Uppsala in 1925. There are several plac
2 CONC es and buildings named after Sten Sture in the town of Arboga in central Sweden, where Sten Sture was elected in 1471, due to the efforts of the local Sture society in the late 19th century. Stureparken was created for the 400th a
2 CONC nniversary in 1871, and an obelisk dedicated to the memory of the election of Sten Sture was erected in the park in 1890.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Trivia===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''An underground restaurant''' in Stockholm, close to the Royal Palace is named after Sten Sture. In 1792 it was a prison, where the murderer of King Gustav III of Sweden was jailed before his execution.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT <br/>
