1 NAME Trotte /Petersson Eka/
2 GIVN Trotte
2 _MARNM Petersson Eka
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF 1333
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1363
1 OCCU Riksråd, Riddare, Känd 1333-1362
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Trotte Pedersson (Eka)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Peter Hinsekindätten, Eka 
2 CONT * Trotte Pedersson , (also Throtto ) mentioned 1333–1362. Knights , governor of Tveta in Småland 1353, national council 1357. He wrote to Benhammar .
2 CONT * Trotte Pedersson belonged to the noblemen who did not abandon Magnus Eriksson during the men's rise against him.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Trotte Petersson (son of Peter Tab. 1). Privy Council. '''Married to Ingegerd Jonsdotter,''' daughter of Johan Magnusson ( Folkungaättens illegitimate branch ) in her 1st marriage. (Married 2nd with Henrik Reventlow )
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Magnus Trotteson,''' Knight. Married 1st with Katarina Olofsdotter daughter of the bailiff Olof djekn and Elin Karlsdotter. Married 2nd before 1404 with Märta Magnusdotter, daughter of the bailiff Magnus Kase and Bengta Bengt
2 CONC sdotter (Oxenstierna)
2 CONT * '''Elin Trottadotter,''' Nun
2 CONT * '''Johan Trotteson'''
2 CONT * '''Unnamed Daughter,''' married to the National Council Lars Björnsson
2 CONT * '''Unnamed Daughter,''' to the gunman Tideke Pinnow
2 CONT * '''Birgitta Trottotter,''' married before 1378 with Kort Görtz
2 CONT
2 CONT == Swedish Diplomatarium's National Archives of Medieval Letters
2 CONT
2 CONT == SDHK No: [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=Trotte+Petersson+Eka&EndastDigitaliserat=false&Fritext=Trotte+Petersson+Eka&AvanceradSok=True&page=3&postid=sdhk_8575&tab=post&FacettState=undefined%3Ac%7C#tab 8575]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1364 August 27, of Issue: Oslo'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus Trottesson sells well-to-do man Ebbe Piik his estate Allatorp in the Fjärås parish in Halland, which his father Mr Trotte Petersson legally acquired from a Nils Larsson for 10 schilling cave.  The issuer and well-off men La
2 CONC rs Sonesson and Nils Gedda seal. - https://lbiiif.riksarkivet.se/sdhk!8575_8575.jpg/full/1000,666/0/default.jpg
2 CONT
2 CONT ==SDHK-nr: [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=revelsta&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=6&postid=sdhk_8218&tab=post#tab 8218]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1362 November 30, of Issue: Enkoping'''
2 CONT . King Magnus imposes the heirs to Magnus Nilsson of Revelsta at a fine of 49 marks within six weeks of service to pay the '''knight Trotte Petersson,''' a full year's land guild of Kaby (in the Simtuna parish), which in King Erik
2 CONC 's time was withheld from Mr. Trotte. - https://sok.riksarkivet.se/dokument/sdhk/8218.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT ==SDHK-nr: [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=magnus+trottesson&EndastDigitaliserat=false&Fritext=magnus+trottesson&AvanceradSok=True&page=4&postid=sdhk_9054&tab=post#tab 9054]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Dating: 1367 March 4
2 CONT
2 CONT Henrik Reventlow, his wife Ingegerd Jonsdotter (illegitimate Folkungaätt) and (stepson) Magnus Trottesson (Ekaätt) admit their debt of 10 marks silver Stockholm weight and 120 Swedish coins ordinary coins to be paid next June 24 t
2 CONC o the knight Magnus Birgersson (Leopard), to which they pledge 11 herb land in Bogesund and 4 herb land in Askrike (?) in (Eastern) Ryds parish and 2 land land in Berga, Haga parish, on condition that they resign these lands to Mr
2 CONC  Magnus and his heirs if the principal sum is not paid to him on time, with fasting and everything else that law and the customary law of the country require. If Mr. Magnus and his heirs were to be impeded in the possession of the
2 CONC se goods, they are granted the right of disposal to the issuer's farm Benhamra.  The issuers and the knight Magnus Håkansson (Leopard), the gunman Finvid Finvidsson seal. - https://sok.riksarkivet.se/dokument/sdhk/9054.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/16789 Trotte Petersson (Eka family)]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Privy Council'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Trotte Petersson, lived another 30 Nov. 1362, there was possibly already 1 sept. 1363 and certainly 27 Aug. 1364. Mentioned 1333, was Chief of the Falkenberg 29 Feb. 1344; was still called May 8, 1348, only "nobilis vir" but wa
2 CONC s knight Feb 2. 1349; was chief of the Varberg on June 18, 1349, was governor of Tveta (Jönk.) Nov. 28; 1353, was Counsel (March 19, 1351) March 6, 1358; is called May 31 and June 11, 1361 the King's "Justice". 
2 CONT
2 CONT G. married Ingegerd Johansdotter (illegitimate Folkungaätt), daughter of the lawyer Johan Magnusson. She was 4 March 1367 round. m. the knight Henrik Reventlow and still lived 14 Jan. 1390th
2 CONT
2 CONT As a castle captain, T. was already in Magnus Eriksson's service in the 1340s, and during the following period up to the beginning of the 1360s, he was often found in the king's immediate surroundings as his fellow sealer, liftman
2 CONC  or trusted servant. It is clear that he had Magnus' confidence, since he held Falkenberg shortly after the peace with Denmark in 1343, when Halland, after prolonged battles, was finally recognized as Swedish, and since then becam
2 CONC e chief of the Varberg, northern Halland's most important stronghold. As far as you can see, he was one of those who remained Magnus Eriksson faithful in the times of adversity. In 1348 he seems to have participated in the king's 
2 CONC campaign to Russia, and it was probably during the battles there that he acquired the knighthood. Then in 1351, during the second campaign of Magnus, the Pope's representative, the Swedish krona granted a large money loan, T. was 
2 CONC one of the absent major men whose bail was promised. In the agreement on this loan he is called for the first time the Council of Ministers, but this letter includes among the members of the Council several persons, who appear to 
2 CONC have been counted only temporarily. It was not until 1358 that he was a sure member of the council. During Erik Magnusson's feud 1356–59 he stood faithfully on Magnus' side and appears in several letters as his pledgee. Two letter
2 CONC s from 1361, issued in the king's name and sealed with his judgment seal, are testified by T. as the king's "justice of justice". This title means, with all certainty, that T. has temporarily held approximately the same position a
2 CONC s the Danish Chancellor (justice) and thus had the right to issue letters of judgment in the King's name and seal them with the judgment seal; who was therefore entrusted to him. It is not known what attitude T. adopted during the
2 CONC  conflict between Magnus and his son Håkan 1361–62. After the reconciliation of the two parties in the spring of 1362, he acted as Håkan's counsel and later, in the name of the latter, also dropped them; in Södermanland. After 136
2 CONC 2 there is no evidence that T. was still alive. It is possible that he was already dead Sept. 1. 1363, when his wife testified that a person paid a debt to her husband, and in any case he must have been dead Aug 27. 1364, when his
2 CONC  son owned and sold land belonging to T. After 1362 there is no evidence that T. was still alive. It is possible that he was already dead Sept. 1. 1363, when his wife testified that a person paid a debt to her husband, and in any 
2 CONC case he must have been dead Aug 27. 1364, when his son owned and sold land belonging to T. After 1362 there is no evidence that T. was still alive. It is possible that he was already dead Sept. 1. 1363, when his wife testified tha
2 CONC t a person paid a debt to her husband, and in any case he must have been dead Aug 27. 1364, when his son owned and sold land belonging to T.
2 CONT
