1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Ragvald Nilsson (Fargalt)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of gunman Nils Ragvaldsson
2 CONT * '''Married:  Ingegärd Karlsdotter (Stjärnbjälke),''' a daughter of Karl Dansson Stiernbielke and Ingeborg Magnusdotter Gregersson oä Folkunge
2 CONT
2 CONT =Geneology=  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''gunman Nils Ragvaldsson'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1- '''Ragvald Nilsson''' was born around 1328. He was district chief in Oppunda district in Södermanland. Married to Ingegärd Karlsdotter, daughter of Karl Dansson (Stjärnjälke) and Ingeborg Magnusdotter (Bjälboätten's illegitimat
2 CONC e branch).
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=  
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Karl Ragvaldsson''' (mentioned 1382–1401).
2 CONT * '''Ingeborg Ragvaldsdotter,''' married to Magnus Elofsson (Tandlaätten)
2 CONT * '''Magnus Ragvaldsson the squire''' (mentioned 1399–1419). Married to ? 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Child''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Ragvald Magnusson (Fargalt)''' (1426–1469) married to Katarina Svensdotter (Pik).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Child''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Peder Ragvaldsson (Fargalt),''' mentioned for the last time in 1499, with him the family seems to have died out on the male side.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Margareta Pedersdotter''' was married to the gunsmith Klas Kyle.
2 CONT * '''Karin Pedersdotter,''' married to her sister's brother-in-law the gunsmith and lawman on Öland Johan Kyle (brother of Klas Kyle).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=15148 Fargalt Family, by the Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Volume 15 (1956), page 367.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Fargalt, conventional, after the coat of arms ("fargalt", i.e. wild boar) formed family name of a medieval royal family, which already early had significant landholdings, gradually came to belong to the high royal family and was m
2 CONC ainly connected to Södermanland. The tinctures of the coat of arms are stated in a painted coat of arms in Tövsala church (Åbo o. Björneb. county) as black and yellow (Hausen, 1882, no. 78, cf. the preface), by Peringskiöld as a b
2 CONC lack fargalt in a field of gold.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family descends from the squire Ragvald Karlsson , who seals in 1334 in Östergötland (DS no. 3084; seal) and in 1345 in Södermanland (DS no. 4011). Judging by the seal, father's name and place of residence, the squire Nils Rag
2 CONC valdsson (sealed in 1342 according to Genealogica 75, fol. 241 v, RA [cf. Register å dokument, Misc. 10: 53 HA: s dep., RA] and 1345, DS no. 3954, also mentioned in 1359, RPB nos. 417, 418) is his son. He had three sons Ragvald, A
2 CONC nders and Magnus Nilsson, of whom the first two were excommunicated in 1358 (RPB no. 365; about Magnus cf. Geneal. 41, fol. 204 v, RA). The first-mentioned squire Ragvald Nilsson (b. c. 1328) was district governor in Oppunda (Söd.
2 CONC ) in 1355 (DS no. 5123) and again in 1361 (RPB 506) and owned Gräfseboda (now Haneberg) and Hedensö (both in Näshulta sn, Söd.). He is particularly known through Birgitta's canonization process (ed. by Collijn), which tells that R
2 CONC agvald had a stroke on Hedensö on 23 Aug. 1374, during which he became distorted in the face and paralyzed in half his body. After it was determined by a roll of the dice that one should call upon the already then considered saint
2 CONC  Birgitta Birgersdotter , Ragvald made a pilgrimage vow, the paralysis began to recede, and he made a pilgrimage to Vadstena, where he was seen "completely healthy, rosy in the face and with a well-fed body". The rumor about the m
2 CONC iracle spread far and wide. He was still alive in 1388 (RPB no. 2378). His wife Ingegerd Karlsdotter was the daughter of the squire Karl Dansson (Stjärnbjälke) and Ingeborg Magnusdotter (illegitimate Folkungaätten). Ragvald had pr
2 CONC obably inherited Hedensö in Österrekarne from his father-in-law. Ragvald Nilsson had a daughter and two sons, the prebendary of Strängnäs Karl Ragvaldsson (mentioned 1382–1401) and the squire Magnus Ragvaldsson(mentioned 1399–1419
2 CONC ). The latter owned Hedensö (SD no. 570) and Gräfseboda (E 176, fol. 11 r, UB), as well as other land in Södermanland, Östergötland and Uppland. He was buried on 6 Feb. 1419 in Vadstena monastery church (in the Vadstena diary he i
2 CONC s then called a squire from Uppland, 'armiger de Uplandia'), together with his wife, who died a short time before him. Their son was the squire Ragvald Magnusson, who is mentioned from 1426 (28 April, perg., RA) and was still aliv
2 CONC e in 1469 (Hist. handl., 16: 1, p. 28, note 68). He owned Hedensö and Västerby in Tumbo (Västerrekarne; Hadorph's large copybook E 73–76, machine folio 191 v; Örnhielm, Dipl. 11, p. 304), as well as Herrborum in Skällviks sn (Ög.)
2 CONC  – probably his wife's inheritance or morning gift (perg. 2 and 19 Jan. 1463, RA) and farms in Västgoeda and Närkingen, which were his wife's inheritance. He was married to Katarina Svensdotter, daughter of the squire Sven Pik (Pe
2 CONC rsonhist. tidskr., 1909, p. 19). They had several children, including the squires, councilors and lawmen Peder Ragvaldsson (B. 1), mentioned 1463–99, and Erik Ragvaldsson (B. 2), mentioned 1463–94, and Karl Ragvaldsson, bailiff at
2 CONC  Turku Castle, mentioned 1463–72 (perg. 2 Jan. 1463, RA; FMU, 4, no. 3487); the coat of arms of the latter is painted in Tövsala church (Hausen, 1873, pp. 28 f., and 1882, preface and no. 78). With Peder Ragvaldsson the family see
2 CONC ms to have become extinct on the male side. He left behind two daughters (Anna Pedersdotter Bielke's family book, fol. 32 v, Riddarhuset; perg. 9 July 1519, RA), Margareta Pedersdotter , gm squire Klas Kyle, and Karin Pedersdotter
2 CONC  , gm (her sister's brother-in-law) squire, finally the lawman on Öland Johan Kyle.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fargalt Fargalt, by Wikipedia] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fargalt is a modern name for a Swedish noble family that had a wild boar, or fargalt, in its coat of arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT The boar in the coat of arms is indicated in the painted coat of arms in Tövsala church as black and yellow, and is indicated by the antiquarian Johan Peringskiöld as a black boar in a field of gold.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family was from Södermanland and came to belong to the High Salvation. The oldest known member of the family is Ragvald Karlsson who in 1334-1335 seals letters with his seal. Judging by the seal, patronymic icon and hometown, 
2 CONC the gunsmith Nils Ragvaldsson is his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils Ragvaldsson had property in Finspånga in Östergötland. He is mentioned in the sources 1342–1349. He is apparently the Nils Ragvaldsson, who
2 CONT together with his sons Ragvald and Anders were excommunicated in 1358 for abuse of a servant of the priest in Skällvik, Hammarkind's district in Östergötland. He also had a third son named Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about Anders Nilsson and Magnus Nilsson. Anders is mentioned in 1356 and possibly 1370. Magnus is mentioned in 1354 and 1360.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragvald Nilsson was born around 1328. He was district chief in Oppunda district in Södermanland in 1355 and again in 1356. He suffered a "stroke" on August 23, 1374 and became "warped in the face and paralyzed in half of the body"
2 CONC . Through a roll of the dice, it was decided that it should be called even then as the holy Birgitta Birgersdotter. Ragvald took a pilgrim's vow and the paralysis began to subside. He made a pilgrimage to Vadstena and became there
2 CONC  "completely healthy, rosy in the face and with a well-born body". No later than 1354, he married Ingegärd Karlsdotter. She was the daughter of Karl Dansson (Stjärnjälke) and Ingeborg Magnusdotter (Bjälboätten's illegitimate branc
2 CONC h).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragvald Nilsson had a daughter and two sons, the prebend in Strängnäs Karl Ragvaldsson (mentioned 1382–1401) and the squire Magnus Ragvaldsson (mentioned 1399–1419). The latter owned Hedensö and Gräfseboda (now Haneberg in Eskilst
2 CONC una) and land in Södermanland. His sister Ingeborg Ragvaldsdotter was married to Magnus Elofsson (Tandlaätten).[1] [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The lawman and Riksdag Erik Ragvaldsson (1463–1494), son of Ragvald Magnusson (Fargalt) (1426–1469) and Katarina Svensdotter (Pik) belonged to the same family. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT With the Riksdag and lawman in Södermanland's law saga, Peder Ragvaldsson (Fargalt), mentioned for the last time in 1499, the family seems to have died out on the male side. He left behind two daughters who married two brothers fr
2 CONC om the Kyle family, Margareta Pedersdotter was married to the squire Klas Kyle, and sister Karin Pedersdotter, was married to her sister's brother-in-law (brother of Klas Kyle), the squire and lawman on Öland Johan Kyle
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources= 
