1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Sven Jonsson/Johansson Halv Lilja=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Armsman Sven Halv-Lilja formerly Jonsson aka Dingelvik'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Johan Halstensson Halv lilja, Till Dingelvik
2 CONT * Sven Jonsson registered at Dingelvik , which was a farmstead , later a manor in (Steneby parish).
2 CONT * [https://arkivkopia.se/bok/runeberg-ladal 7) '''Skrefs Dyngowiik 1449. Owned then by the squire Sven Jonsson, and thus an ancient manor. See a morning gift letter issued by him at Grinstad parish, No. 8. The same Sven Jonsson is
2 CONC  mentioned in a letter on 10 February. 1494 (printed by Lilljegren after the original in v. Engeströmska library, 1819. page 41). published by Magnus Bonde's widow Kadrin Ingwarsdotter. Owned in 1563 by the well-to-do wife Karin, 
2 CONC who owned both Dingelviks farms. Lästviken, Åsen, Högen, Hölgerud, Hafden, Bågen, etc. fl. In 1577, Nils Månsson at Bro was the owner of this: 1664 wife Margareta Svenske: 1783 court junior Reuter: 1809 horse master Sam. Tile: 182
2 CONC 7 Carl Pet. Åberg: 1845 crown county in Näs district of Wermland JF Wennerlöf: 18-17 baron St. Gyr and countess Ramel, born Lewenhaupt.''']
2 CONT * Sven Jonsson's seal is half a lily in Ginbalk. One seal is illegible and 3 are missing completely. Sven Jonsson has the coat of arms together with the Høgfrælseætten Halsten Peterson (half a lily) married to Margreta (also calle
2 CONC d Märta) Knutsdatter, (coat of arms Three Roses) she was born in the early 14th century, maybe around 1310/1315?. A family with many parallel branches, without knowing for sure about these.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sven Johansson (Jonsson) (half lily) was a squire living in Sweden. He signed up for Dingelvik. He married on October 5, 1449 at Stola '''Ingeborg Bytesdotter,''' probably the daughter of Tore Byting . (Stola is a manor in Strö 
2 CONC parish in Lidköping municipality 15 km northwest of Lidköping),
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Ragnhild Svensdotter (half lily)''' Married Gudmund Torbjörnsson Gumsehuvud. Ragnhild (Ragnilla) Svensdatter inherited Sundal and Walbo and married Gudmund Torbjørnsson Til Forstena, their only daughter Anna (also called Ragn
2 CONC hild) Gudmundsdatter married Hæggatorp and became the matriarch of the actual Forstena clan, which had 5 sons in edge inheritance of 3 full farms, besides at Dal - Ulfstorp and Forstena, Lunden, Nygård and/or more.
2 CONT * '''Karin Svensdotter (half lily)'''  Married Mårten Jønsson and inherited the Dingelvik Farm. Daughter Karin (1) Svensdatter was born in Dingelvik, probably married to a Mårten Jonsson (who is in Dingelvik), then it follows almo
2 CONC st a tradition that the women in Dingelvik with as many as 4 Karins and a couple of Margareta, up to Karin the lV Svenske in the late 17th century.
2 CONT * '''Botilla Svensdotter (half lily)''' Married Væpner Olav Amundson Holter, Stjerne under Sparre. Daughter Bothild (a) Svensdatter (born c.1450) got her inheritance in Norway (so they owned farms in Norway! but where?) (she was m
2 CONC arried to squire Olav Amundsen, Holter in Fenstad parish in Nes på Romerike in Akershus (Arms Star under spars).
2 CONT
2 CONT =Swedish Diplomatarium's Main Catalog of Medieval Letters=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sdhk?EndastDigitaliserat=false&SDHK=25585&TrycktUtgava=true&TrycktRegest=true&Brevtext=true&Extratext=true&Sigill=true&Original=true&MedeltidaAvskrifter=true&MedeltidaRegest=true&EftermedeltidaAvskrif
2 CONC ter=true&EftermedeltidaRegest=true&AvanceradSok=False&page=1&postid=sdhk_25585&tab=post#tab '''SDHK-nr: 25585''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1449 October 6''' 
2 CONT * '''Place of issue: : Stola'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Content
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000006829153230&photo_id=6000000191056009827&position=0 '''Sven Johansson in Dingelvik (in Steneby parish) gives his wife Ingeborg Bytingsdotter as a gift of farms an
2 CONC d estates in the parishes of Bråland, Gestad, Örs and Grinstad on Dal.''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources:''' Original: Or. perg. Printed, Lignell (1852), pp. 25 f. with trans. Dalslands Diplomatarium (1996), no. 127. Norra Dingelvik is an old farm, whose name in 1449, when it was owned by the squire Sven Jonsson, was writt
2 CONC en Dyngowiik
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sdhk?EndastDigitaliserat=false&Innehall=G%c3%a5rd&TrycktUtgava=true&TrycktRegest=true&Brevtext=true&Extratext=true&Sigill=true&Original=true&MedeltidaAvskrifter=true&MedeltidaRegest=true&Eftermedeltid
2 CONC aAvskrifter=true&EftermedeltidaRegest=true&AvanceradSok=true&page=1844&postid=sdhk_33066&tab=post#tab '''SDHK-nr: 33066''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1494 February 9'''
2 CONT * '''Place of issue; Sjögerås''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Content
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Katarina Ingvarsdotter, Magnus Bonde's widow ,''' allows her cousin Lindorm Björnsson, lawman in Västergötland, to redeem her farm Höja in Varnums sn in Värmland on her behalf. '''The farm was pawned by her father to Sven Johan
2 CONC sson in Dingelvik for 20 sall of malt.''' Sealed by the issuer together with his (half) brother Hans Svensson, prefect of Gudhem.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Original:  Or. perg. RA 0101  images, Printed with transl. in Dalsland's Diplomatarium (1996), no. 193.
2 CONT * [https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Bonde_nr_11 TAB 17 - '''Magnus Bonde (son of Filip Bonde, Tab. 16 ). Died before 1493. Squire. Married to Katarina Ingvarsdotter, daughter of squire Ingvar Michelsson (boat) and Ingeborg Abj
2 CONC örnsdotter (Sparre over a leaf)''' There Children were, Tord Bonde. Knight and councillor & Anna Magnusdotter. Married in 1507 to Krister Bengtsson ( Lilja of Värnäs ).]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?ValdSortering=DatumStigande&PageSize=100&Sokord=forn%C3%A5sa&EndastDigitaliserat=False&FacettLimits=zfSYjA%3A0&AvanceradSok=False&page=15&postid=sdhk_10962&tab=post '''SDHK no: 10962''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1377 March 5'''
2 CONT * '''Place of issue: Sundby in Forsa parish''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Content
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Håkan Halstensson issues a letter regarding the land exchange that Mr. Lidinvard Haraldsson made on behalf of the issuer in Forsa with the issuer's mother Mrs. Märta Knutsdotter and his brother in the presence of Bishop Tomas i
2 CONC n Växjö, Johan Gregersson and Mr. Peter in By, regarding both the fathers, mothers and mother's morning gift. The executor divides the previously undivided estate with his mother and his brother Knut Halstensson in such a way that
2 CONC  he transfers to them his entire property in Fornåsa in Boberg's county in exchange for their entire property in Lower and Upper Götala in Styra parish.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The issuer seals together with the knights Ragvald Magnusson and Ingevald Anundsson and with Mr. Jakob i Forsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources - Post-medieval transcripts:  Transcript - under the heading "Bytes bref opå Fornusum in Boberss heradh medh Nidhra och Öffra Götalum etc. 1377." - in Lars Eriksson Sparre's copybook B 15, fol. 11r, RA 0202'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sven_Jonsson_(halv_lilja) Sven Jonsson (half lily), by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sven Jonsson (half lily) died around 1484, was a gunsmith living in Sweden . He was the son of Johan Halstensson ( Halsten Petersson's family ). Sven Jonsson signed up for Dingelvik , which was a salvation farm , later a manor in 
2 CONC ( Steneby parish ). He married on 5 October 1449 [ 1 ] at Stola to an Ingeborg Bytesdotter, probably the daughter of Tore Byting.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Ragnhild Svensdotter (half lily)'''
2 CONT * '''Karin Svensdotter (half lily)'''
2 CONT * '''Botilla Svensdotter (half lily)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT (. 6.Oct. 1449 - Sven Jonsson (Johansson) in Dingelvik in Stenby parish (Västre Götaland) in Sweden gives his wife Ingeborg Toresdatter Byting til Morgengave),
2 CONT
2 CONT It reads like this: Everyone who sees or hears this letter greets I Sven Jonsson in Dyngevik, the reverend armed with God, announcing in this letter that I have given my dear lovely wife Ingeborg Bytingsdotter as a gift tomorrow o
2 CONC n the right due date dessa nedan nemda goods at Dal.
2 CONT
2 CONT First, a farm called Tveten with a deserted plot of land under it lying in Brålanda parish. (Dalsland, Western Götaland)
2 CONT
2 CONT The next farm called Bodane in Gestad parish (Dalsland, Västre Götaland) And a farm called Hagen in Grinstad parish (Dalsland, Västre Götaland) with underlying øde plot, and my whole halft in Søder byn in the same Grinstad parish 
2 CONC lying with all its ægor and ægondeler in wet and dry, honorable and distant and nothing of what rightfully belongs to me excepted. And this morning I have shot and shafted her according to the law on the prescribed date of occurre
2 CONC nce with these 12 men.
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Jon Andrisson -'''
2 CONT *  2. '''Olaf Andrisson -'''
2 CONT *  3. '''Peder ...... -''' 
2 CONT * 4. '''Lasse Jonsson -''' 
2 CONT * 5. '''Sven Ormsson -''' 
2 CONT * 6. '''Henrik Skrivare -''' 
2 CONT * 7. '''Hans Ingersson -''' 
2 CONT *  8. '''Tord Ingemarsson -'''
2 CONT *  9. '''Bjørn Laurisson -''' 
2 CONT * 10. '''Enar Håkansson -''' 
2 CONT * 11. '''Gunulf i Svalehede -'''
2 CONT *  12. '''Birger Birgerson -''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT As the law book shows that if we have children and the children inherit together, then this fake morning gift should be given to the mothers, not to the fathers. If we don't have children, which God forbid, then whoever lives the 
2 CONC longest will keep the gift of tomorrow. And that this shall be fixed and inviolable under her and her right heirs, as is prescribed, I ask these honorable men for their seals. which one is: 1. Bengt Gøstasson, lecturer in Skara - 
2 CONC 2. Ingvar Mickelsson - 3. Jøns Andrisson - 4. Armsman Henrik Skriver, to hang together with my seal under this letter, as written on Stola.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sven Jonsson (ca.1420 - ca.1484?) and Ingeborg Toresdatter (ca.1425 - ?) Byting from Stola manor in Kållan, was thus married on 5 Oct. 1449.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sven Jonsson's sigil is half a lily in Ginbalk. One seal is indistinct and 3 are completely missing. Sven Jonsson has the coat of arms together with the Høgfrælseætten Halsten Peterson (half Lilja) married to Margreta (also called
2 CONC  Märta) Knutsdatter, (coat of arms Tre Rosor) she was born in the early 14th century, maybe around 1310/1315?. A genus with many parallel branches, without knowing these for sure.
2 CONT
2 CONT The widow Margrete (Märta) Knutsdatter (coat of arms Tre Rosor) inherited according to her will 29 Sept. 1358, the then childless aunt Ramfrid Bengtsdatter Leion (Hafridsätten), her husband and children died in the Black Death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margreta Knutsdotter was remarried to Magnus Uddolfsson (see SDHK-nr. 7497, 1358 - 24 Dec. - written on Berg - , Arvid Petersson (brother of Halsten Petersson) assigns to Honorable man Magnus Uddolfsson his farm in "Hollasio" and 
2 CONC Åby mill against his farm in Ramkvilla and his heirloom castle after it stands Håkan (it's probably a typo for Halsten) Petersson
2 CONT
2 CONT Halsten Peterson (Halv Lilja) (the older one) died between 24 Febr. 1358 and 29 Sept. 1358.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halsten Peterson was King Magnus Eriksson's (1316 - 1374) confidant. Halsten's coat of arms can be found on a famous folding table in Luneberg (Germany). Halsten's weapon was a lily in Ginbalk Halsten (carried a divided lily ginba
2 CONC lkwise in Upper field (half a yellow lily in red upper field)
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogist JR Raneke is sure to misread the coat of arms as a cross-divided lily, as the photo (of the clapboard) is telling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sven Jonsson has around 5 mantals in Dal Adeln.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingeborg Toresdatter Byting's morning gift was a sixth of Sven's inheritance at Dal, with Dyngovik (Dingelvik) as the seat.
2 CONT
2 CONT A later generation had problems with a daughter marrying below her status. The brother solved this by donating a whole farm to his sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sven Jonsen's inheritance was at least 3 x 5 mantal and the daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Ragnhild (Ragnilla) Svensdatter''' inherited Sundal and Walbo and married Gudmund Torbjørnsson Til Forstena, their only daughter Anna (also called Ragnhild) Gudmundsdatter married Hæggatorp and became the matriarch of the 
2 CONC actual Forstena clan, which had 5 sons in edge inheritance of 3 full farms, besides at Dal - Ulfstorp and Forstena, Lunden, Nygård and/or more.
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2. '''Daughter Bothild (a) Svensdatter''' (born c.1450) got her inheritance in Norway (so they owned farms in Norway! but where?) (she was married to squire Olav Amundsen, Holter in Fenstad parish in Nes på Romerike in Akershus 
2 CONC (Arms Star under spars).
2 CONT
2 CONT * 3. '''Daughter Karin Svensdatter''' was born in Dingelvik, probably married to a Mårten Jonsson (who is in Dingelvik), then it follows almost a tradition that the women in Dingelvik with as many as 4 Karins and a couple of Marga
2 CONC reta, up to Karin the lV Svenske in the late 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Hans Petersson mentioned (letter) in 1484 in Dingelvik and was evidently married to a daughter of Sven Jonsson (Halv Lilja) (also written as Johansson) and Ingeborg Toresdatter (Byting). From the text of the letter it appears th
2 CONC at Hans Petersson buys back the farm from Oluf Amundsson (Stake?), which his father-in-law Sven Jonsson previously sold to Erik Mattsson Kagge (to Rössvik). How it had ended up with Oluf Amundsson is not clear, but it could be his
2 CONC  wife Karin Mattsdotter Kagge's property, she was the widow of Gregers Jönsson. An interesting wording in the letter reads "SVEN JONSSON, may God have mercy on his soul, who was then the right guardian of the noble estate" One get
2 CONC s the impression that he sold on behalf of someone incompetent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a medieval deed SDHK-nr:3453- - 1484- 05-04 - Rössvik - it says this: Hans Petersson in Dingelvik (in Steneby parish) about property in Husbyring parish, which Oluf Amundsson and his wife, widow of Gregers Jönsson, gave him etc
2 CONC . Sealed by Mr. Birger (Svensson) parish priest in Husby-Rekarne, and the squire Anders Eriksson district chief in Östrrekarne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Which of Sven and Ingeborg's children should have been married to Hans Petersson is incomprehensible, as there are only 3 children mentioned after Sven and Ingeborg, 1. Karin, 2. Ragnhild and 3. Bothild (Botilla?) and none of them
2 CONC  mentioned as married to Hans Petersson, so this must be wrong?
2 CONT
2 CONT At: https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sven_Jonsson_(halv_lilja) - it says that Sven Jonsson was the son of Johan Halstensson (Halstein Petersson's family), (the Johan who was Sven's father I believe must have been born approx. 1390?).
2 CONC  But according to Utter, this Johan was a son of Halsten Petersson and Marta Knutsdotter, but (this must be a younger Halsten Petersson, who should have been born around the 1360s?), and his father again Peter (Halstensson) born c
2 CONC .1330 /35, whether this was the Peter Halstensson mentioned in Träcksta from and including 18 May 1362 to and including 7 May 1401 is probably somewhat uncertain, but he again (assumed?) was the son of Halsten Peterson - Halv Lilj
2 CONC e) ant.born about 1290/1300? - 1358) married to Margreta (Märta) Knutsdatter (c.1300 - lived in 1377), daughter of Knut (father's name unknown, but previously he was called Jonsson av Mørby). and his wife Christina Bengtsdatter Le
2 CONC jon. Sven Jonsen (Johansson) used the same half lily in Ginbalk, (not on Tverren split lily,) that Halstein Peterson's family used.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halsten Petersson (ca.1290/1300? - 1358 and Margareta (Märta) Knutsdatter (ca.1300/1310? - after 1362, also had children Knut Halstensson mentioned 1377 and 1385 and Håkon Halstensson Mentioned 1377 and 1385.
2 CONT
2 CONT (PS: An interesting Liljeförende Knight Halsten Petersson is mentioned in the Smålen Njudung and in Flundre herad by the Gøta river 1329 - 1357. King Magnus Eriksson's confidant.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1355, SOFI mentions a "Necklace in Tracksta", and on 24 Febr. In 1358 we find Halsten in THRÆGSTA, as permanent resident in Vadsbro (Södermanland). Halstein Peterson therefore died between 24 February 1358 and 29 Septe
2 CONC mber. 1358, and not as certain sources state in 1357.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1362, Magnus Finvidsson gives a total of 1:0 in Ella and HEBy in GÅSINGE parish as a gift to his wife (Margareta) Marta Knutsdatter. (Was she married 3 times?)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1384 Ragnvald Magnusson (Håkon Tunasson's ætt) assigns O:O:10 to Håkan Halstensson (Halsten Petersson's ætt)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1385, Halstein Petersson's son Knut Halstensson fulfills his mother Margareta (Marta?) Knutsdatter (Tre Rosor)'s will by donating, with his brother Håkan Halstensson's consent, 1/2 of EKA and land in outer HEBY, as well as addi
2 CONC tional land in HEBY - in GÅSINGE the sock of St. Andreas prebend in Strængenes.
2 CONT
2 CONT (If Peter (Halstensson) in Tracksta (ca. 1330 - did not die until 1401, was the brother of Knut and Håkan, then he should probably be mentioned here?
2 CONT
2 CONT From and including 18 May 1362 (until 7 May 1401?) we find a Peter in Träcksta, (Vadsbro socken, Södermanland, near Stockholm) mentioned 8 times, mostly as regulars. This is ant. Peter Halstensen Half Lily. (Yellow Lily on Red Fie
2 CONC ld).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1401, an interesting land acquisition concerns land in Fyrby in Blacksta parish and in Vik in Vadsbro parish, which Ingebor in Kjallsta and her son Nils (Gregersson) had sold to Peter (Halstensen) in Träcksta, who was Peter's r
2 CONC ight Odel (byrdh). The seller was obviously Ingeborg Nilsdatter (married to Greger Anundsson), sister of Knight Karl Nilsen (Färla), who was murdered on 13 June 1381 by Bo Jonson Grip (died 1386) in front of the high street in the
2 CONC  Gråmunkernes monastery church.
2 CONT
2 CONT But Karl was no Färla, he had a split shield, which at that time was carried by the Hammestad family.
2 CONT
2 CONT From where Peter Halstensen got his (byrdh's right) inheritance right to buy land in FYRBY in Blacksta and in Vik in Vadsbrosocken is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the same period that PETER (HALSTENSON) was in TRACKSTA, we also find a Johan, mentioned in 1393-1422-1424, who may be the father of Nils Johnsen (Johansson) in Tracksta, who in 1435 settled on land in Eknes, Flinkestad and
2 CONC  VIK in Vadsbro socken, which he had bought from Håkan Halstensson and his sister Cecilie Halstensdatter (this must be a younger Håkon Halstensson than the one mentioned 1346? -1398?). A Håkan (Hacon) is mentioned in 1398, and cou
2 CONC ld be? a brother,? (or an unknown son?) to Peter Halsteinsson (the older one who should have been born c.1330)
2 CONT
2 CONT SEE - https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halsten_Peterssons_æt Here a slaughter table has been set up: But it seems completely hopeless in terms of time between the generations: Halsten Petersson (Halv Lilja) (married to Märta Knutsdot
2 CONC ter, three Rosor), who most recently must have been born early in the 14th century, is listed as the father of Johan Halstensson (Halv Lilja), he should probably have been born around 1330/35?, and then cannot be, as it says, the 
2 CONC father of Sven Jonsson (Halv Lilja), born c.1420 - died a little before 1484. Living at Frälsegarden Dingelvik in Stenby socken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Halstensson (1330/35?) is also the father of Halsten Johansson (Halv Lilja), who in turn is said to have been the father of a Knight Håkan Halstensson (Halv Lilja), mentioned as an adult already in 1346 - mentioned until 138
2 CONC 0 and married to Bengta , daughter of Riksråd Sigge Magnusson. (But Håkan Halstensson, married to Bengta, was the brother of Knut Halstensson (Halv Lilja) Mentioned in 1377 and 1385, and was the child of Halsten Petersson and Märt
2 CONC a Knutsdotter (Tre Rosor). (I am not sure which of these 2 mentioned Håkan Halstensson , who during the civil war in 1364 had his estates confiscated by King Håkon Magnusson, who gave it to Nils Bengtsson, Lion-faced (who is menti
2 CONC oned as living from 1315 to 1364, Östra herad in Småland, Østgøtaland).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dingelvik Dingelvik, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dingelvik is a place in Bengtsfors municipality , Västra Götaland county , located in Steneby parish . The development was defined by Statistics Norway as a small town in 1990 and was deregistered as such in 2020 due to the fact t
2 CONC hat the number of residents was below 50.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dingelvik Farm'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm in Dingelvik has been known since the Middle Ages, and the squire Sven Jonsson (half lily) (died around 1484), signed himself to Dingelvik . The farm was inherited within the Halsten Petersson family , and passed to the o
2 CONC lder Forstenas family (ram's head), and after a division into Norra and Södra Dingelvik, during the 17th century, northern Dingelvik was owned by the Svinhufvud family , while southern Dingelvik was owned by the Ahlefeldt family .
2 CONT
2 CONT A man building on Södra Dingelvik was built around 1848-1850 by the then owner Baron Henry de Reverony St. Cyr.
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm is today owned by the Kling family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halsten_Peterssons_%C3%A4tt Halsten Petersson's Family, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Halsten Petersson's family ,''' also called half lily , is a contemporary conventional name for a Swedish medieval squire family that took its name from the progenitor, knight and bailiff Halsten Petersson. The family died out 
2 CONC before Sweden's Riddarhus was founded in 1625, which is why it was never introduced there as nobility . The members of the family wore a red scotch, with half a golden lily laid in beams in the upper field.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''History'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Two land exchanges from 1377 and 1380 give good insight into the mutual kinship of several members.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family Tree'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Halsten Petersson, knight (1355),''' landowner and bailiff. His origin is unknown, but a (half) brother is mentioned, Arvid Petersson, who carried a spar in the coat of arms. 1336 and 1338 goods as thanks for faithful service
2 CONC . he was given by King Magnus Eriksson but participated in King Erik Magnusson 's rebellion against his father, and is mentioned among Erik's five pledges in 1357, but is stated to have died on March 1, 1358. [ 3 ] '''Married Märt
2 CONC a (Margareta) Knutsdotter ( Tre Rosor till Mörby ), daughter to Knut (Tre Rosor till Mörby) and Kristina Bengtsdotter in Haneström, daughter of the lawman in Västergötland Bengt Hafridsson .''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1- '''Johan Halstensson'''
2 CONT >>>* 1- '''Halsten Johansson.''' According to Peder Månsson Utter (1566-1623) in ( Collectanea genealogica ), Halsten Johansson carried a fragmented shield with the upper half of a split (not split) lily gilt in the upper field. A
2 CONC ccording to the same source, Halsten was the father of Håkan Halstensson. 
2 CONT >>>>>* 1- '''Håkan Halstensson,''' knight, named 1346-1380. [ 8 ] Married to Bengta, daughter of the knight and councilor Sigge Magnusson ( Vinstorpaätten , Håkan Tunesson's family ) and Ramborg. 
2 CONT >>>>>>>>* 1 '''NN Håkansdotter,''' married to Ulf Bengtsson ( Sparre of Hjulsta and Ängsö ), parents of, among others, Bishop Sigge in Strängnäs.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>* 2 - '''Sven Jonsson (half lily) til Dinglevik,''' Steneby, died about 1484, was a gunsmith and signed up to Dingelvik , which was a salvation farm , later a manor in ( Steneby parish ), and married on 5 October 1449 [ 11 ] at
2 CONC  Stola with an Ingeborg Bytesdotter, the daughter of Tore Byting . 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>'''Three children are known:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>* '''Ragnhild Svensdotter (half lily)
2 CONT >>>>* '''Karin Svensdotter (half lily)''' to Dinglevik, Steneby.'''
2 CONT >>>>* '''Botilla Svensdotter (half lily)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2 - '''Thord Halstensson'''
2 CONT * 3 - '''Knut Halstensson (half lily)''' was married to Katarina Guttormsdotter, daughter of Guttorm Toresson (split      shield) and Inga Gregersdotter ( Malstaätten )
2 CONT * 4 - '''Håkan Hallstensson''' During the civil war in 1364, had his estates confiscated by King Håkan Magnusson , which Håkan gave to Nils Bengtsson (Lion-faced, Bengt Nilsson's family)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Bo J. Theutenberg writes in his book "Doktor Johannes Copp von Raumenthal, ett livsöde i reformationens spår" published in 2003=
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Bo J. Theutenberg writes in his book "Doktor Johannes Copp von Raumenthal, ett livsöde i reformationens spår" published in 2003 which is part of the Skara Stiftshistoriska sälläpä writing series "no. 14" pp. 150 and 151 ab
2 CONC out Hallsten Petersson and his origin. There are several documents in which the children of Abjörn (Sparre) and Lucia Matsdotter (the older bulls) leave their farms to their blood relative Hallsten Petersson, who bore the coat of 
2 CONC arms a split yellow lily in a red field. The 1329 – 1357 (or 1358) mainly in Småland Njudung knight Hallsten Petersson, King Magnus Eriksson's fiduciary, shows in his coat of arms a briseur of lily - the coat of arms, a change of 
2 CONC an original lily - coat of arms. This points to the existence of an old lily-bearing family in Njudung, which has had contact with a lily-bearing family in the area around Halleberg and Hundeberg along the Göta River. It is probab
2 CONC ly one and the same lily - family, where the different members brandish their lily - weapons in different ways. The farms in Fors and Åsbräcka parishes in Flundre district were only a few miles from the church in Gärdhem, where th
2 CONC e lily stones are found, and to which Mrs. Lucia's sister Bengta Matsdotter donated gifts in her will in 1338, probably because there were old family ties between the farms and the families in Gärdhem and the farms and families in
2 CONC  Flundre district. The lily-bearer Hallsten Petersson was thus blood-stained with the Abjörnska Sparreätten in Flundre Härad. The close ties are particularly shown by the fact that Hallsten Petersson's brother Arvid Petersson hims
2 CONC elf carried Sparre in his coat of arms. In the work itself, it can be stated that the lily-bearer Hallsten Petersson was the grandson of the knight Abjörn (Sparre) and wife Lucia Matsdotter, as was probably the brother Arvid Peter
2 CONC sson (Sparre). This is proven by the fact that Abjörn and Lucia's daughter Margareta Abjörnsdotter, when she sells her share in the farm Berg in Fråstad in 1341 (DS 3591), mentions Hallsten Petersson as her blood relative ( discre
2 CONC to viro consanguineo meo Halstano Pætherson) it is further proven by Margareta's will from 1342 (DS 3614), in which she directly mentions him as her sororio, i.e. as her nephew (item sororio meo Halstano duas marchas et tantum fra
2 CONC tri suo Aberno). Thus Hallsten Petersson, like probably his brother Abjörn (Aberno), possibly a misspelling for the above-mentioned brother Arvid, belonged to the Abjörnska Sparreätten in Flundre district on the mother's side. The
2 CONC  one of the sisters who is Hallsten Petersson's mother is most likely Ingrid Abjörnsdotter, who in addition to her marriage to the Norwegian Håkan Bårdsson must have been married to Hallsten Petersson's father, Peter NN from Njudu
2 CONC ng, who may be assumed to have carried the same coat of arms as the son Hallsten . Here a connection appears between the lily family and one of the old Abjörn Sparreättar, which group consisted of the Sparre family of Tofta, of th
2 CONC e Abjörn Sparreätten in Flundra county and of Abjørn Ulfsson's Sparreätt from Finnveden. It is especially a letter dated 21 September 1340 (DS 3520) that points out Ingrid Abörnsdotter (Sparre) as the mother of the knight Hallsten
2 CONC  Petersson. In this, a man named Olof Gren commits himself to, according to the Västgötalagen, assign to Hallsten Petersson all the estates in Fråstad that Håkan Bårdsson's wife Ingrid Abjörnsdotter had inherited from her aunt Ben
2 CONC gta Matsdotter. This indicates that it is Hallsten Petersson who takes over the inheritance from his mother. The mentioned Olof Gren is probably identical to the Olof Ebbesson (Sparre) who appears in a letter dated in Uplanda near
2 CONC  Kinnekulle on April 9, 1367 (RBP no. 761) and who, according to a letter on October 4, 1366 (DS 7428), was brother to Enegisle Ebbesson (Sparre), who on 29 June 1364 (DS 7020) appears as district chief in Rädväg's district. The f
2 CONC ather of the brothers Olof Ebbesson and Erengisle Ebbesson, Ebbe Gren (Sparre) acts closely to the Sparre - family known as the Unnesönnernas Sparre family, which in turn is close to Jöns Abjörnsson's Sparre over the sjøblads - fa
2 CONC mily (so the Forstäna family).
2 CONT
