1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Barbro Ingelsdotter Roos af Hjelmsäter=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Ingel Knutsson Roos af Hjelmsäter and Ingeborg Salomonsdotter Rahm 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Roos_af_Hjelms%C3%A4ter_nr_51#TAB_92 Noble Family Roos, AF HJELMSÄTER no. 51, by Adelsvapen Genealogies]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancient Salvation Family, Introduced in 1625.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family, which in age can compete with the oldest purely Swedish noble families still alive, forms a branch of the Norwegian Hafthorson family and is the only one of Norway's old noble families that still survives. Its oldest k
2 CONC nown ancestor Jon Raud Ivarsson is assumed to have been the grandson of the Norwegian king Håkon Håkonsson's cousin Jon af Suderheim, who lived around 1240. It already branched out in the 14th century into the two Swedish families
2 CONC , of which one of the genealogists has been called Roos af Ervalla, while the other has been named Roos af Hjelmsäter ever since its introduction to the knight's house. The common origin of these families - they used to be conside
2 CONC red two separate families despite the common family mark, a red five-petaled rose in a gold field - has been determined by KH Karlsson in an essay in Personhistorisk Tidskrift in 1909. In Norway, the baronial family died out at th
2 CONC e end of the 15th century with Tymme Tymmesson, son of Jon Hafthorsson's brother, knight Sigurd Hafthorsson's grandson. - The family was matriculated at the Knights' House in Finland on 02/07/1818 under No. 2 among nobles, but exp
2 CONC ired there as early as 1832.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''TAB 90 - Ingel Knutsson , (son of Knut Knutsson, table 8).''' Mentioned among the saviors liable to service in Värmland in 1528 (GI Reg.). Sat in the court over the rebellious Västgöta lords in 1529. Processed about farms in Vä
2 CONC rmland in 1533. (GI Reg.) Married to Ingeborg Salomonsdotter , daughter of Salomon Thorstensson in Strand in Värmland and sister of the commander of Åland Torsten Salomonsson (Ram), who was ennobled by King Gustaf I 1525-04-01.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dordi Ingelsdotter,''' 
2 CONT * '''Knut Ingelsson.''' Hövidsman. (son of Ingel Knutsson, table 90), to Näs. 'In 1564 was chieftain of a banner of Swedish jacks and was left as commander in possession of Lyckå castle in Blekinge. Still alive in 1575. For what h
2 CONC e left the said castle when those who were there, without any need or coercion, burned down the castle, 1564-10-20 by King Erik XIV's committee sentenced from honor and life, which later he however had to keep. Later complained ab
2 CONC out Göran Persson, that he gave him shield and helmet (nobility), which he had inherited from honest birth and family, even though he, both in the time of King Gustaf I and later, after his ultimate fortune in the alleged long feu
2 CONC d probably proven.'
2 CONT * '''Ingel Ingelsson.'''
2 CONT * '''Margareta Ingelsdotter.'''
2 CONT * '''Carin Ingelsdotter.'''
2 CONT * '''Otto Ingelsson.'''
2 CONT * '''Barbro Ingelsdotter,''' married to Olof Larsson Ramstad
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/St%C3%A5rck_nr_233 Adelsvapen Genealogies]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Noble Family Stårck no. 233'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Knighted 1632-08-12, confirm. 1634-09-10. Introduction 1635. Family extinct on the sword side in 1973.
2 CONT The family descends on the mother's side from an old Värmland salvation family to Ramstad in Ny parish, whose oldest known member Anders Jonsson is mentioned in 1414. After Ramstad was sold by his grandson Ingemar Persson to the b
2 CONC ishop's chair in Skara in 1513, this farm was restored by another branch of the family. A member of this, Olof Larsson till Ramstad, who lived in 1555 and was married to Barbro Ingelsdotter (Roos af Hjelmsäter), had a son Lars Olo
2 CONC fsson. In addition to Ramstad Västbro, this owned Bro parish and others. farms in Värmland as well as Rynninge in Längbro parish and Näs (now Hjälmarsnäs) along with four farms in Våtsta in Stora Mellösa parish, all in Närke, but 
2 CONC sold or exchanged all these farms except Ramstad and Västbro before the end of the 16th century. Lars Olofsson was knighted by King Johan on 23 August 1589 in Reval, was still a lieutenant in 1598, but before 1602 master of the ho
2 CONC rse for a standard horseman and also a court eunuch to Duke Carl, and died on April 23 (25?) 1606 and is buried in Nikolaikyrkan in Örebro, where his tombstone with his image and coat of arms carved is found. He was childless in h
2 CONC is marriage to Estrid Olofsdotter Bååt, of an old Värmland salvation family, born 1553, died 1647. His nephews Nils and Jon Perssöner adopted the maternal uncle's coat of arms unchanged (a stork holding a snake in its beak). This 
2 CONC was then recognized by the nobility in 1632 to Jon Persson, who was then dead, and his descendants, who after the coat of arms adopted the family name Stårck, which in older times was written Sturk or Sturck. He was childless in h
2 CONC is marriage to Estrid Olofsdotter Bååt, of an old Värmland salvation family, born 1553, died 1647. His nephews Nils and Jon Perssöner adopted the maternal uncle's coat of arms unchanged (a stork holding a snake in its beak). This 
2 CONC was then recognized by the nobility in 1632 to Jon Persson, who was then dead, and his descendants, who after the coat of arms adopted the family name Stårck, which in older times was written Sturk or Sturck. He was childless in h
2 CONC is marriage to Estrid Olofsdotter Bååt, of an old Värmland salvation family, born 1553, died 1647. His nephews Nils and Jon Perssöner adopted the maternal uncle's coat of arms unchanged (a stork holding a snake in its beak). This 
2 CONC was then recognized by the nobility in 1632 to Jon Persson, who was then dead, and his descendants, who after the coat of arms adopted the family name Stårck, which in older times was written Sturk or Sturck. In 1776 the Pomerania
2 CONC n government councilor Carl Ludvig von Storck, who belonged to a German royal family, became a naturalized Swedish nobleman Stårck, under the pretext that he was a descendant of the Swedish family of that name through his great-gr
2 CONC andfather Johan Storck, born 1617, died 1683, who is said to have been chancellor in Osnabrück. This is not found in the older genealogies of the knight's house, but was later inserted as the son of the progenitor Jon Persson Står
2 CONC ck. As a kind of proof, it is stated that "Johan Jönsson Stårck is found to have signed the cession of customs freedom among the nobility in 1644". However, this is a mistake, because in the said document it says: Jon Jönsson Lind
2 CONC h (Lind af Hageby). To identify the chancellor Johan Storck with the progenitor's real son, the lieutenant of the Älvsborg regiment Jon Stårck, is hardly possible. Jon Stårck is mentioned for the last time in the regiment's rolls 
2 CONC at the muster in Vedum on January 23, 1644, but not in a list of the regiment's sick in Landskrona the same year or at the muster in Vedum on August 29, 1645. As it is highly unlikely that he burning war would have gone to Germany
2 CONC  and settled there, and there is no evidence that after the muster in 1644 he would have been transported to another regiment, one should probably take for granted the knight's house genealogy's statement that he "died in Skåne". 
2 CONC As far as we know, no proof of the relationship between the Stårck family and the German von Storck family has been provided. The last mentioned family, which at the elevation to royal status on 29 November 1753 received a complet
2 CONC ely different coat of arms from the Swedish one but not in a list of the regiment's sick in Landskrona the same year or at the muster in Vedum on 29 August 1645. Since it is highly unlikely that he would have gone to Germany and s
2 CONC ettled there in the midst of a raging war, and there is no proof because he would have been transported to another regiment after the muster in 1644, one should probably take for granted the knight's house genealogy's statement th
2 CONC at he "died in Skåne". As far as we know, no proof of the relationship between the Stårck family and the German von Storck family has been provided. The last mentioned family, which at the elevation to royal status on 29 November 
2 CONC 1753 received a completely different coat of arms from the Swedish one but not in a list of the regiment's sick in Landskrona the same year or at the muster in Vedum on 29 August 1645. Since it is highly unlikely that he would hav
2 CONC e gone to Germany and settled there in the midst of a raging war, and there is no proof because he would have been transported to another regiment after the muster in 1644, one should probably take for granted the knight's house g
2 CONC enealogy's statement that he "died in Skåne". As far as we know, no proof of the relationship between the Stårck family and the German von Storck family has been provided. The last mentioned family, which at the elevation to royal
2 CONC  status on 29 November 1753 received a completely different coat of arms from the Swedish one and there is no evidence that he would have been transported to another regiment after the muster in 1644, one may take for granted the 
2 CONC knight's house genealogy's statement that he "died in Skåne". As far as we know, no proof of the relationship between the Stårck family and the German von Storck family has been provided. The last mentioned family, which at the el
2 CONC evation to royal status on 29 November 1753 received a completely different coat of arms from the Swedish one and there is no evidence that he would have been transported to another regiment after the muster in 1644, one may take 
2 CONC for granted the knight's house genealogy's statement that he "died in Skåne". As far as we know, no proof of the relationship between the Stårck family and the German von Storck family has been provided. The last mentioned family,
2 CONC  which at the elevation to royal status on 29 November 1753 received a completely different coat of arms from the Swedish one1 - the lineage from Sweden seems not to have been known at the time - still survives in large numbers in
2 CONC  Germany, although not through the government councilor Carl Ludvig von Storck-Stårck, whose family branch died out in the last half of the 19th century.
2 CONT
