1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bodil Thurgotsdatter, queen consort of Denmark'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Thrugot Ulfsen Fairskin, Jarl of Denmark and Þórgunnr Vagnadóttir 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriages==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married firstly '''BJØRN''' ---.  m secondly (before 1086) '''ERIK Svendsen,''' illegitimate son of SVEND II Estridsen & his wife --- (Slangerup Cyprus 10 Jul 1103, bur Cyprus).  He succeeded in 1095 as ERIK I "Ejegod/the Good" 
2 CONC King of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Project MedLands Denmark Kings==
2 CONT
2 CONT ERIK Svendson, illegitimate son of SVEND II Estridsen King of Denmark & his wife his mistress.(Slangerup[489]  Paphos, Cyprus [10 Jul] 1103, bur Cyprus).  His brother King Knud appointed him Jarl in Sjalond in [1080][490].  On the
2 CONC  release of his brother Olav from prison in Flanders, Erik retired with his wife to Sweden[491].  He succeeded his brother in 1095 as '''ERIK I “Ejegod/the Good” King of Denmark.'''  The Chronicon Roskildense records that "Hericus
2 CONC  Bonus frater eius" succeeded after the death of "Olavus rex" and reigned for eight years[492].  Snorre records that "Eirik the Good, King Svein's fourth son" succeeded King Olav and ruled for eight winters[493].  Saxo Grammaticus
2 CONC  records that he undertook a pilgrimage to Jerusalem in 1102 as penance for having killed four warriors but died en route in Cyprus[494].  The Chronicon Roskildense records that "Hericus…cum uxore sua Botilda" left for Jerusalem i
2 CONC n 1103 but died on arriving "ad Kyprum"[495].  According to Saxo Grammaticus, King Erik was "unusually tall…matched by corresponding strength" but "only the power of lust and the rage of intemperance marred this brilliance of body
2 CONC  and mind"[496].  The Liber Vitæ of Durham names "Eiric rex Danorum Botild regina"[497]. 
2 CONT m (before 1086) as her second husband, '''BODIL Thorgunnasdatter,''' widow of BJØRN, daughter of THRUGOT Ulfsen & his wife Thorgunna Vagnsdatter (-Mount of Olives, Jerusalem 1103, bur St Mary’s Church in Joseph’s Valley).  She is 
2 CONC named as the wife of Erik by Saxo Grammaticus, who also gives her father's and paternal grandfather's names[498].  A charter dated 1194/95 names “Botildis Regina ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia orta” as mother of “Kanutum…Dux Dano
2 CONC rum et Rex Slavorum”[499].  The Chronicon Roskildense records that "Hericus…cum uxore sua Botilda" left for Jerusalem in 1103 but died on arriving "ad Kyprum"[500].  Saxo Grammaticus records that she accompanied her husband on pil
2 CONC grimage to Jerusalem and died there[501].  The Liber Vitæ of Durham names "Eiric rex Danorum Botild regina"[502]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Erik I & his wife had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''KNUD "Lavard/the Lord" (Roskilde 12 [Mar/Apr] [1096]''' murdered Haraldsted Skov, near Ringsted 7 Jan 1131, bur Ringsted).  Named as his father's legitimate son by Saxo Grammaticus[503].  The Chronicon Roskildense 
2 CONC names "Kanutum" as the son of "Hericus Bonus…ex legitima"[504].  He was appointed Count in Sønderjylland [Schleswig] in 1115 by his uncle Niels King of Denmark.  He led Danish counter-attacks against Heinrich Fürst in Wagrien, who
2 CONC  had devastated southern Schleswig, but made peace by agreeing to buy the disputed land from Heinrich[505].  He mediated the dispute between his two half-brothers Harald and Erik by ordering an equal partition of their patrimony[5
2 CONC 06].  Helmold records that "principatus Sclavorum" was transferred to "Kanutum, filium Herici regis Danorum" after "Zventepolch filius…Zvinike" was killed[507], dated to [1128/29].  The Chronicon Roskildense records that "Magnus u
2 CONC nicus filius Nicholai regis" killed "Kanutum filium Herici regis" in 1130[508].  Saxo Grammaticus records that he was murdered by his cousin Magnus, (later Magnus I King of Denmark), his cousin Henrik and his brother-in-law Haakon
2 CONC  Sunnivasson, jealous of his success and ambitions[509].  The Annales Colbazienses record that "Canutus" was killed "1130 VII Id Jan" by "Magno filio Nicolai Regis"[510].  The burial records of Ringsted record that "sanctus Kanutu
2 CONC s dux Dacie, filius regis Erici Egothæ in Cypro" was killed "VII Id Jan…in silva Harelstæthæ" in 1130 by "Magno filio Nicolai tunc regis Danorum" and was buried at Ringsted[511].  He was canonised in 1170.  m ([1116])''' INGEBORG 
2 CONC Mstislavna,''' daughter of MSTISLAV I Vladimirovich Grand Prince of Kiev & his first wife Christine of Sweden (-after 1131).  Snorre names "Harald Valdemarson's other daughter, sister to Malmfrid…Ingebjorg" as wife of "Canute Lava
2 CONC rd, a son of the Danish king Eirik the Good and grandson of King Svein Ulfson"[512].  Fagrskinna names “Málfridr ok Ingibjörg” as daughters of “Harald konungr” & his wife “Kristinar, dóttur Inga konungs Steinkelssunar”, adding tha
2 CONC t Ingeborg married “Knútr lávardr, bródir Eiriks eimuna”[513].  Morkinskinna records that “Haraldr Valdimarsson´s second daughter…Ingibjorg” married “King Knútr lávardr”[514].  A charter dated 1194/95 names “Ingiburgh filia Rizlav
2 CONC i…Ruthenorum Regis et Cristinæ Reginæ…filia…Ingonis Suevorum Regis et Helena Reginæ” as mother of “Waldemarum Regem”[515].  Her marriage was arranged by her maternal aunt Margareta Queen of Denmark to improve relations between the
2 CONC  Swedish and Danish royal families[516].  Saxo Grammaticus records that she warned her husband of a possible plot against his life in 1131 but that he ignored her[517].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Knud & his wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * a)         MARGRETE Knudsdatter.  Snorre names (in order) "the Danish king Valdemar…and daughters Margaret, Christina and Catherine" as the children of "Canute Lavard" & his wife, recording that Margrete married "Stig Hvitaled" 
2 CONC and that their daughter was "Christina, married to the Swedish king, Karl Sorkvison, and their son was king Sorkver"[518].  Fagrskinna names (in order) “Valdimarr konungr ok Kristin ok Katerin or Margareta” as children of “Knútr l
2 CONC ávardr, bródir Eiriks eimuna” and his wife, noting that Margrete married “Stigr hvitaledr”, father of “Nikoláss ok Kristinar er átti Karl konungr Sverkissunr”[519].  “Ericus III Rex Danorum” donated property to the monks “in Othen
2 CONC si” by charter dated 7 Dec 1141, witnessed by “… filiabus Canuti Christina, Katherina, cum Domina Margareta et Botilda…”[520].  m as his first wife, STIG Tokesen [Hvide], son of --- (-killed in battle Gedbæk, near Viborg 1151).
2 CONT
2 CONT * b)         KIRSTIN Knudsdatter ([1118]-).  Snorre names (in order) "the Danish king Valdemar…and daughters Margaret, Christina and Catherine" as the children of "Canute Lavard" & his wife[521].  Fagrskinna names (in order) “Vald
2 CONC imarr konungr ok Kristin ok Katerin or Margareta” as children of “Knútr lávardr, bródir Eiriks eimuna” and his wife[522].  Morkinskinna records that King Magnus married “Kirstín, the daughter of Knút lávardr and the sister of the 
2 CONC Danish king Váldimarr” but adds that he sent her back to Denmark[523].  Snorre refers to the wife of King Magnus as "a daughter of Knut Lavard…sister of the Danish King Valdemar" and records that her husband sent her back to Denma
2 CONC rk[524].  Her husband accused her of betraying his plan to attack Niels King of Denmark and sent her back to Denmark[525].  “Ericus III Rex Danorum” donated property to the monks “in Othensi” by charter dated 7 Dec 1141, witnessed
2 CONC  by “… filiabus Canuti Christina, Katherina, cum Domina Margareta et Botilda…”[526].  m (1133, divorced 1133) MAGNUS Sigurdsson King of Norway, illegitimate son of SIGURD "Jorsalafare/the Crusader" Joint King of Norway & his wife 
2 CONC his mistress Borghild Olavsdatter ([1115]-killed in battle near Holmengrå, Svinesund 12 Nov 1139, bur Oslo, Hallvards Church).
2 CONT
2 CONT * c)         KATARIN Knudsdatter.  Snorre names (in order) "the Danish king Valdemar…and daughters Margaret, Christina and Catherine" as the children of "Canute Lavard" & his wife[527].  Fagrskinna names (in order) “Valdimarr konu
2 CONC ngr ok Kristin ok Katerin or Margareta” as children of “Knútr lávardr, bródir Eiriks eimuna” and his wife[528].  “Ericus III Rex Danorum” donated property to the monks “in Othensi” by charter dated 7 Dec 1141, witnessed by “… fili
2 CONC abus Canuti Christina, Katherina, cum Domina Margareta et Botilda…”[529].  Saxo Grammaticus records that “Nucleti...filio eius Priszlauo” settled in Denmark, was baptised, and married “Waldemari sororem”[530].  As Snorre records t
2 CONC he marriages of her sisters Margrete and Kirstin, Prislaw’s wife must presumably have been Katarin.  m ([1159]) PRISLAW [von Mecklenburg] Herr zu Laaland [Lolland], son of NIKLOT Fürst der Obotriten & his wife --- (-[1165/75]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT * d)         [BOTILDA (-after 7 Dec 1141).  “Ericus III Rex Danorum” donated property to the monks “in Othensi” by charter dated 7 Dec 1141, witnessed by “… filiabus Canuti Christina, Katherina, cum Domina Margareta et Botilda…”[5
2 CONC 31].  The wording of the witness list suggests that all four ladies were daughters of Knud, but no other reference has yet been found to a daughter of his named Botilda.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT * e)         VALDEMAR Knudson (posthumously 14 Jan 1131-Vordingborg 12 May 1182, bur Ringsted).  Snorre names (in order) "the Danish king Valdemar…and daughters Margaret, Christina and Catherine" as the children of "Canute Lavard"
2 CONC  & his wife[532].  He succeeded his cousin in 1157 as VALDEMAR I "den Store/the Great" King of Denmark. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' Denmark Kings - http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/DENMARK.htm#ErikIdied1103B
