1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Anastasia Yaropolkovna''' (1074 - January 3, 1158) - princess/duchess of Minsk, daughter of Prince Yaropolk Izyaslavich of Volyn and Kunigunda Orlamyundskaya, wife of Prince Gleb Vseslavich of Minsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born as a princess of Volyn and Turov, princess Polotsk and Minsk, from Rurik VIII generation
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mentions'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the chronicles, Anastasia is mentioned only once, in the year of her death. This mention is interesting primarily from the point of view of the history of the church. Under the year 1158, the chronicler indicated that on Januar
2 CONC y 3, at the age of 84, at 2 a.m., Yaropolkovna, the widow of Prince Gleb, died, outliving her husband by 40 years, and was buried on the 4th in the Assumption Cathedral of the Kyiv-Pechersk Monastery next to the shrine of St. Theo
2 CONC dosius Pechersky with her husband. She bequeathed 5 villages with servants and in general all her property to the monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The time when the marriage between Anastasia and Gleb Vseslavich was concluded is not known exactly, but it can be calculated. It is known that after the murder of Yaropolk in 1086, Kunigunda left for Germany, taking her youngest 
2 CONC daughter with her, where she got married. Anastasia, on the other hand, remained in Russia, probably because she was already in the care of her husband, or at least was engaged. At that time, marriage was given from the age of 12,
2 CONC  which allows us to indicate 1086 as the approximate time of the wedding. However, sometimes the beginning of the 1090s is indicated in this capacity. L.V. Alekseev believed that Anastasia was married to Gleb at the beginning of 1
2 CONC 073, which caused a conflict between Izyaslav Yaroslavich and his younger brothers, but in 1073 Anastasia was not yet born.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sons:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Rostislav (d. c. 1165), Prince of Minsk, Prince of Polotsk
2 CONT * Volodar (died after 1167), Prince Gorodtsovsky, Prince Minsky, Prince Polotsk
2 CONT * Vsevolod (d. 1159/1162), Prince Izyaslavsky, Prince Strezhevsky
2 CONT * Izyaslav (d. 1134)
2 CONT
2 CONT Historian A. V. Nazarenko doubted that Rostislav was Gleb's son by Anastasia, suggesting that he could have been the son from an unknown first marriage. The reason for the doubt was that in this case, Rostislav married his second 
2 CONC cousin, which was not allowed by the church, but sometimes happened. However, the origin of Rostislav's wife has not been unequivocally established, so Rostislav may well be the son of Anastasia.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00141420&tree=LEO
