1 NOTE Flanders, Matilda of 1031-1083
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=a8b36c54-0bec-4346-af5f-1cb1503d0439&tid=9784512&pid=-639109555
1 NOTE Forfedrene går tilbake til bl a Julius Caecar, King Ramses, Nebukadneser II, Adam og Eva.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Name Prefix:<NPFX> Queen of England Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8XHZ-T2
2 CONT !Title: Countess Of FLANDERS and Queen of England. "Royal Ancestors" by Michel Call, 1989, Chart # 11420, # 11672.
2 CONT _UID1ACA46275B91E94097E246BC8E34ABF54373
2 CONT Buried at Holy Trinity Abbey, Caen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: RC 81, 89, 140, 141; K and Q of Britain; Kraentzler 1062,1157, 1218, 1241, 1265, 1342, 1346, 1350, 1371; Coe; A. Roots 162, 169;Guizot's "France"; AIS; Pfafman; Davis.
2 CONT Duchess of Normandy and Queen of England.
2 CONT She was the ancestress of English rulers down to the present time(1991).
2 CONT Two death dates: 2 or 3 Nov. 1083. No mention in all this of a secondhubby as claimed by Kraentzler in line 1371. The chart, source unknown, saysWilliam was her second husband. Her first was Gerbod de Flandre, Advocate of the Abbe
2 CONC y St. Bertin and St. Omer. Their daughter, Gundreda of Chester,married William de Warenne, First Earl of Surrey. Sile Rice in her novel, TheSaxon Tapestry, says Matilda had a daughter, Gundrada, by an unnamed firsthusband. All oth
2 CONC er sources say Gundred/Gundrada was the daughter of William I, and birth dates given would so indicate.  Also, referring to Matilda (page354), "And her own daughter was the wife of Earl Warenne"--indicating thisdaughter was not th
2 CONC e daughter of William I.
2 CONT AIS: Matilda of Flanders, born about 1031, Flandre, Belgium; died 2Nov. 1083, Caen, France.
2 CONT K-1350: Mathilde de Frandre, "Maid of Flandre". Born about 1031,Flandre, Belgium; died 2 Nov. 1082, Caen, Calvados, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Kraentzler 1371.
2 CONT K: Gerbod de Flandre, Advocate of the Abbey St. Bertin and St. Omer. K. and a chart, source unknown, are the only sources I've seen sayingMatilda of Flanders was married to anyone besides William the Conqueror.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guizot says:
2 CONT Matilda, daughter of Baldwin "The Debonnair," one of the most powerful lords of the day, was "beautiful, well-informed, firm in the faith, amodel of virtue and modesty." And William asked for her hand in marriage. But she replied,
2 CONC  "I would liefer be veiled nun than given in marriage to abastard." (The above quotes apparently are from a chronicle of the period, butGuizot does not name the chronicler).
2 CONT William was hurt, but he decided he would have to gain more fame andpower before trying again. Guizot says, "Some years later, being firmlyestablished in Normandy, dreaded by all his neighbors, and already showing some foreshadowi
2 CONC ngs of his design upon England, he renewed his matrimonialquest in Flanders, but after so strange a fashion that, in spite of contemporary testimony, several of the modern historians, in their zeal, even at sodistant a period, for
2 CONC  observance of the proprieties, reject as fabulous the storywhich is here related on authority of the most detailed account amongst all the chronicles which contain it."
2 CONT This chronicle relates: "A little after that Duke William had heardhow the damsel had made answer, he took of his folk, and went privily to Lille,where the Duke of Flanders and his wife and his daughter then were. He enteredinto t
2 CONC he hall, and, passing on as if to do some business, went into thecountess's chamber, and there found the damsel daughter of Count Baldwin. Her tookher by the tresses, dragged her round the chamber, trampled her under foot, anddid 
2 CONC beat her soundly. Then he strode forth from the chamber, leapt upon hishorse, which was being held for him before the hall, struck in his spurs, andwent his way. At this deed was Count Baldwin much enraged; and when matters hadthu
2 CONC s remained a while, Duke William sent once more to Count Baldwin to parleyagain of the marriage. The count sounded his daughter on the subject, and she answered that it pleased her well. So the nuptials took place with verygreat j
2 CONC oy. And after the aforesaid matters, Count Baldwin, laughing withal,asked his daughter, wherefore she had so lightly accepted the marriage she hadaforetime so cruelly refused. And she answered that she did not know the duke sowell
2 CONC  then as she did now; for, said she, if he had not great heart and highemprise, he had not been so bold as to dare come and beat me in my father'schamber."
2 CONT Guizot does not discount this story, noting it is contained in several chronicles, and he adds, "... as to the ruffianly gallantry employed byWilliam to win his bride, there is nothing in it very singular, considering thehabits of
2 CONC  the time, and we meet with more than one example of adventures if not exactly similar, at any rate analogous."
2 CONT
2 CONT [Have found no other reference to this tale elsewhere.]
