2 CONT
2 CONT Ordulf, Duke of Saxony
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordulf (sometimes Otto) (c. 1022–28 March 1072) was the duke of Saxony from 1059, when he succeeded his father Bernard II, until his death. He was a member of the Billung family.
2 CONT
2 CONT His entire reign was occupied by wars with the Wends. He was allied with Denmark, which alliance he strengthened by marrying (1042) Wulfhild (died 24 May 1071), the daughter of Olaf II of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a second wife in Gertrude of Haldensleben, daughter of a Count Conrad, who was imprisoned in Mainz in 1076 and died 21 February 1116. By Wulfhild he had a son named Magnus who succeeded him. He had a son named Bernard by Ge
2 CONC rtrude who died of a fall from a horse at Lüneburg on 15 July in some unknown year. Ordulf himself is buried the church of Saint Michael at Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Another name for Ordulf was Otto of Brunswick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordulf was married a second time, with Gertrud of Nordmark who died in 1116.
2 CONT
2 CONT BILLUNG DYNASTY:
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary ruling dynasty in Saxony in the 10th and 11th centuries. It was founded by Hermann Billung, who in 936 received from the German king (and future emperor) Otto I a march, or border territory, on the lower Elbe River to 
2 CONC be held against the pagan Slavic Wends. In 961 Otto granted Hermann the Saxon ducal title, which on Hermann's death (973) passed to his son and thereafter to his descendants, who continued the struggle against the Slavs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the family extended its conquests toward the Oder River, thus preparing these territories for Christianization, their lands consisted of only the northeastern part of the former stem duchy of Saxony. Hermann's son Bernard
2 CONC  I (d. 1011) obtained guarantees of the special privileges and customs of the Saxons from the emperor Henry II; Bernard II (d. 1059) obtained similar guarantees from the emperor Conrad II. Both Bernard II and his son Ordulf (d. 10
2 CONC 72) had to defend their territories against the encroachments of Adalbert, archbishop of Bremen. The family came to embody the Saxon national resentment toward the Salian kings and emperors Henry III and particularly Henry IV, who
2 CONC  wanted to reestablish imperial authority in Saxony. In August 1106, with the death of Magnus Billung, the family died out.
2 CONT
2 CONT Noted events in his life were:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: Duke of Saxony, 1059. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikopedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordulf (auch Otto genannt) (* 1022; † 28. März 1072) aus der Familie der Billunger war Herzog von Sachsen von 1059 bis 1072.
2 CONT
2 CONT Er folgte seinem Vater Bernhard II. nach dessen Tod im Jahre 1059 als Herzog. Seine Regierungszeit war beherrscht vom Kampf gegen die Wenden, der zusammen mit den christlichen Dänen geführt wurde. Die Heirat Ordulfs mit der norweg
2 CONC ischen Prinzessin Wulfhild stärkte dieses Bündnis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordulf wurde in der St.-Michaelis-Kirche in Lüneburg beigesetzt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nachkommen [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordulf war zweimal verheiratet, mit beiden Frauen hatte er einen Sohn. Seine erste Frau war seit November 1042 Wulfhild von Norwegen († 24. Mai 1071), eine Tochter des Königs Olav II. Haraldsson. Beider Kind war sein Nachfolger, d
2 CONC er spätere Herzog Magnus. Eine zweite Ehe schloss er mit Gertrud von Haldensleben, einer Tochter des Grafen Konrad und Witwe eines Friedrich (wohl von Formbach), die 1076 in Mainz gefangen gesetzt wurde und am 21. Februar 1116 sta
2 CONC rb. Beider Sohn war Bernhard, der am 15. Juli eines unbekannten Jahres in Lüneburg nach einem Sturz vom Pferd starb.
2 CONT
2 CONT Weblinks [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * genealogie-mittelalter.de
2 CONT
