1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Lars Björnsson=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lawspeaker (Lagman) of Västergötland.
2 CONT * He held this supreme judicial office from approximately 1366 to 1382.
2 CONT * According to Bo J. Theutenberg’s analysis of medieval Swedish lawman succession, Lars Björnsson likely inherited his high-noble status through his mother, connecting him to the Algotsson or Ulvåsa dynasties. The bjälke (bar) her
2 CONC aldry associated with his paternal line is identified as an ancient Scandinavian marker linked to the Hvide clan, indicating a deep, landed warrior ancestry in Västergötland. Read the full analysis at [https://theutenberg.org/vast
2 CONC ergotlands-lagman-algotssoner-och-folkungar/Lawmen – Algotssöner and Folkungar]
2 CONT * bjälke, se även Lars Björnssons ätt
2 CONT
2 CONT =Lars Björnsson bjälke Geneology Chart=
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000225647841999&&size=large}}  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://theutenberg.org/vastergotlands-lagman-algotssoner-och-folkungar/ Lawmen – Algotssöner and Folkungar]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the mid-14th century, the office of magistrate in Västergötland passed from both the Algotssönernas family and the Folkunga family. No more magistrates would bear the lion or the griffin head of the Algotssönernas family. It wa
2 CONC s at this time, the mid-14th century, or more precisely in 1364, that the last king of the Folkunga family, Magnus Eriksson, finally lost control of his kingdom to his nephew Albrekt of Mecklenburg (king 1364 – 1389). Taking into 
2 CONC account the tradition that magistrates have some form of family relationship to each other, one can think about the shift that occurred around 1366 when Lars Björnsson (a beam ) took office as magistrate of Västergötland. Lars Bjö
2 CONC rnsson was magistrate for a long time, namely between about 1366 and 1382. The beam coat of arms is old. It was mentioned earlier that the Danish Hvideätten, with roots dating back to the 11th century, used shields with different 
2 CONC numbers of dividing lines in their heraldry : e.g. three, five or seven times divided shield (see image no. 5). If there is an even number of horizontal dividing lines, the coat of arms is called a beam or beams , e.g. one , two o
2 CONC r three beams etc. In Sweden, the beam coats of arms are also considered to be among the oldest coats of arms, as are the spar and divided shield coats of arms, which were used by the family that came to be known as Night and Day.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Image 11. After the fall of the Folkunga family in Sweden at the Battle of Gataskogen in 1364, when King Magnus Eriksson lost control of the kingdom to his nephew Albrekt of Mecklenburg, a change took place in the office of magist
2 CONC rate in Västergötland. After the last gryphon- wearing magistrate of the Algotssönernas family, Algot Bengtsson (1344 and still 1358), the grandson of magistrate Algot Brynolfsson, a magistrate from the bjälke -wearing family grou
2 CONC p took over, namely Lars Björnsson (1366 -1382). There were probably close family connections between different bjälke -wearing people in Småland and Västergötland during the 14th century. In some way, based on experience, there m
2 CONC ust also be a connection between magistrate Lars Björnsson ( a bjälke ) and the lion- and gryphon- wearing magistrates. The seal above (HiSig 3:651) with the beam coat of arms and the inscription “S KAROLI INGELDRISVN” belonged to
2 CONC  the one in Bottnaryd in Mo härad in Småland in 1347 mentioned Karl Ingemundsson, who was married to NN (witch Ragnhild) Siggedotter, a daughter of Sigge Djäkn and Margareta Tomasdotter ( grip head ). It is Karl Ingemundsson and N
2 CONC N Siggedotter's daughter Ingrid Karlsdotter ( a beam ), who together with her husband Udd Matsson ( beamwise set lily ) at Vinstorp become the progenitors of the so-called actual Vinstorp family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As early as the end of the 13th century and the beginning of the 14th century, people with the coat of arms of a beam (lines dividing the shield) appear, especially in Småland and Västergötland. One such person was the cognatic an
2 CONC cestor of the so-called actual Vinstorpa family, Karl Ingemundsson (a beam) in Bottnaryd in Småland, father of Ingrid Karlsdotter in Vinstorp. She was married to Udd Matsson of the lily-lily-balk family. It is Udd Matsson and Ingr
2 CONC id Karlsdotter who become the ancestors of the lily-balk (beam) -bearing Vinstorpa family , which owned Gamlegården in Böne parish in Redvägs district. This heraldic description should help us understand how the family's coat of a
2 CONC rms came about, that is, by combining the coats of arms of two families in the shield. The family's seat was later moved to Vinstorp, formerly Tummatorp, by Lake Vinsarpsjön. Another person carrying a beam is Lars Björnsson, who w
2 CONC as elected as the governor of Västergötland around 1366. It is difficult to precisely map out the connections between beam- bearing people at this time. However, it is likely that an original connection should have existed, which 
2 CONC assumption is supported by the heraldic rule system and its structure. Perhaps the coat of arms of two beams , three beams, etc. was only a refinement of the basic coat of arms of a beam. 40 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
