0 @N6000000002188018950@ NOTE Canute V of Denmark
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Canute V of Denmark (Danish: Knud V Magnussen; born c. 1125 - August 9, 1157) was a King of Denmark and a co-regent with Sweyn III and Valdemar I between 1146 and 1157.
1 CONT
1 CONT Canute was the son of King Niels' son Magnus, who had slain Canute Lavard. After the abdication of Eric III in 1146, the magnates of Jutland declared him king and for the following years he tried in vain to defeat his rival Sweyn 
1 CONC III on Zealand. Canute participated in the 1147 Wendish Crusade. In 1151 he was expelled from Denmark and his request for help from Frederick Barbarossa only resulted in a compromise which made him the inferior co-regent of Sweyn.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, a secret co-operation with Valdemar, the son of Canute's father's old enemy, led to an 1154 alliance which made them sole rulers of the kingdom, forcing Sweyn to flee. After a new war, a final compromise in 1157 made Canu
1 CONC te the ruler of Zealand. During the peace banquet (the Bloodfeast of Roskilde) he was killed, allegedly by one of Sweyn's warriors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nothing sure is known about his person and character. In 1156, he married Helena of Sweden. His half-sister Sofia of Minsk married Valdemar I, who avenged him the same year at the Battle of Grathe Heath by killing Sweyn.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article does not cite any references or sources. (January 2007)
1 CONT Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unverifiable material may be challenged and removed.
1 CONT Canute V
1 CONT Born: c. 1125 Died: August 9, 1157
1 CONT Regnal titles
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Eric Lamb King of Denmark
1 CONT 1146-1157
1 CONT with Sweyn Grathe &
1 CONT Valdemar the Great Succeeded by
1 CONT Valdemar the Great
0 @N6000000000945053647@ NOTE Folkungaätten er, siden Johannes Messenius (1616), en vanlig betegnelse for den ætt 
1 CONT som omfattet kongene i Sverige 1250-1364, i Norge 1319-1387 og i Danmark 1376-1387. S.
1 CONT Bolin viste i 1935 at Messenius' terminologi viler på en opptegnelse fra slutten av 1400-tallet
1 CONT etter eldre kilder fra Varnhems kloster. Disse har senere forsvunnet, og forekommer kun i
1 CONT 1500-talls avskrifter. Ifølge disse stammer Birger jarl fra personer med fornavnet Folke. Dette
1 CONT gjør også visse 1200-talls stormenn som var opprørske mot Birger jarl og hans sønner. Disse ble
1 CONT i samtiden kalt ?Folkungar?.
1 CONT I henhold til disse 1400-talls opptegnelsene stammer Folkungaætten fra hedningen
1 CONT Folke Filbyter. Hans tre sønner var Ingemund, Halsten og Ingevald.
1 CONT Ingevald ble far til en Folke ?grossus? (den tjocke i henhold til den eldste oversettelsen
1 CONT av Olaus Petris).
1 CONT Folke den Tjockes sønn, Bengt Snivil, ble i henhold til samme fortegnelse far til jarlene
1 CONT Birger (Brosa) og Karl (den døve), samt til Magnus Minneskiöld.
1 CONT De to eldste av disse generasjoner er ikke kjent fra andre kilder, om man bortser fra A.
1 CONT Schücks påpekning fra 1952 om at navnet Ingevald forekommer på en runesten ved Bjälbo
1 CONT kirke i Östergötland.
0 @N6000000007328839772@ NOTE Uppges av bl a Olaus Petri att vara Folkungaättens stamfader, men är
1 CONT antagligen en sagofigur (Bra bøckers lexikon)
0 @N6000000002322298441@ NOTE His succession to his father's lands and title led to a conflict with his
1 CONT brother Bohemond de Hauteville in 1085. Roger, a weak ruler, in spite of the
1 CONT support of the pope and of count Roger, presided over the gradual
1 CONT disintegration of the duchy of Apulia. The quarrel with his brother continued
1 CONT until 1095, when Bohemond left on crusade.
0 @N6000000002176653718@ NOTE His succession to his father's lands and title led to a conflict with his
