0 @N6000000003495151379@ NOTE
1 CONT Matilda of Flanders (c. 1031 – 2 November 1083) was Queen consort of the Kingdom of England and the wife of William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Consort of England
1 CONT 25 December 1066 - 2 November 1083
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the daughter of count Baldwin V of Flanders, and Adela Capet (1000-1078/9), daughter of Robert II of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda was known for her formidable temper.
1 CONT
1 CONT William is said to have courted Matilda for seven years, from the time he met her when she was 14, to their wedding when she was 21. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda was the founder of the Holy Trinity Abbey, where she was buried.
1 CONT ======
1 CONT
1 CONT A spoiled young lady accustomed to speaking her mind and getting her way, the 4'2"-tall (Britain's smallest queen) Matilda (or "Maud") told the representative of William, Duke of Normandy (later king of England as William the Conq
1 CONC ueror), who had come asking for her hand, that she was far too high-born (being descended from King Alfred the Great of England) to consider marrying a bastard. When that was repeated to him, William, all 5'10" of him, rode from N
1 CONC ormandy to Bruges, found Matilda on her way to church, dragged her off her horse (some said by her long braids), threw her down in the street in front of her flabbergasted attendants, and then rode off. After that, she decided to 
1 CONC marry him, and even a papal ban (on the grounds of consanguinity) did not dissuade her. They were married in 1052.
1 CONT
1 CONT There were rumors that Matilda had been in love with the English ambassador to Flanders, a Saxon so pale he was nearly an albino, named Brihtric (but nicknamed "Snow"), who was already married. Whatever the truth of the matter, ye
1 CONC ars later when she was acting as Regent for William in England, she used her authority to confiscate Brihtric's lands (without even any formal charges, much less a trial) and throw him into prison, where he died under suspicious c
1 CONC ircumstances consistent with poisoning.
1 CONT
1 CONT When William was preparing to invade England, Matilda outfitted a ship, the Mora, out of her own money and gave it to him. For many years it was thought that she had something to do with creating the Bayeux Tapestry, but historian
1 CONC s no longer believe that; it seems to have been commissioned by William's half-brother Odo, Bishop of Bayeux, and made by Saxons in Kent.
1 CONT Matilda bore William ten children, and he was believed to have been faithful to her, at least up until the time their son Robert rebelled against his father and Matilda sided with Robert against William. After she died, in 1083 at
1 CONC  the age of 51, William became tyrannical, and people blamed it on his having lost her. She was buried at St. Stephen's in Caen, Normandy (then, France now), where William was also eventually buried. Years later, their graves were
1 CONC  opened and their bones measured, proving their physical statures.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Edith of Wessex
1 CONT
1 CONT Succeeded by:
1 CONT Edith of Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilda_of_Flanders"
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Family 1 : Gherbod Advocate of the Abbey of SAINT BERTIN
