0 @N6000000001540763929@ NOTE Matilda of Flanders
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Matilda of Flanders
1 CONT Queen consort of the English; Duchess of Normandy (more...)
1 CONT Consort 25 December 1066 ? 2 November 1083
1 CONT Consort to William I the Conqueror
1 CONT among othersIssue
1 CONT Robert II Curthose
1 CONT William II Rufus
1 CONT Adela, Countess of Blois
1 CONT Henry I Beauclerc
1 CONT Royal house House of Normandy
1 CONT Father Baldwin V, Count of Flanders
1 CONT Mother Adela Capet
1 CONT Born c. 1031
1 CONT
1 CONT Died 2 November 1083 (aged c. 51)
1 CONT
1 CONT Burial St. Stephen's, Caen, Normandy
1 CONT Matilda of Flanders (c. 1031 ? 2 November 1083) was Queen consort of the Kingdom of England and the wife of William I the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the daughter of count Baldwin V of Flanders, and Adèle (1000-1078/9), daughter of Robert II of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT At 4'2" tall, Matilda was Britain's smallest adult queen, according to the Guinness Book of Records. According to legend, Matilda (or "Maud") told the representative of William, Duke of Normandy (later king of England as William t
1 CONC he Conqueror), who had come asking for her hand, that she was far too high-born (being descended from King Alfred the Great of England) to consider marrying a bastard. When that was repeated to him, William, rode from Normandy to 
1 CONC Bruges, found Matilda on her way to church, dragged her off her horse by her long braids, threwher down in the street in front of her flabbergasted attendants, and then rode off. Another version of the story states that William ro
1 CONC de to Matilda's father's house in Lille, threw her to the ground in her room (again, by the braids), and hit her (or violently shook her) before leaving. Naturally Baldwin took offense at this but, before they drew swords, Matilda
1 CONC  settled the matter. [1] by deciding to marry him, and even a papal ban (on the grounds of consanguinity) did not dissuadeher. They were married in 1053.
1 CONT
1 CONT There were rumours that Matilda had been in love with the English ambassador to Flanders, a Saxon named Brihtric, who declined her advances. Whatever the truth of the matter, years later when she was acting as Regent for William i
1 CONC n England, she used her authority to confiscateBrihtric's lands and throw him into prison, where he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT When William was preparing to invade England, Matilda outfitted a ship, the Mora, out of her own money and gave it to him. For many years it was thought that she had something to do with creating the Bayeux Tapestry (commonly call
1 CONC ed La Tapisserie de la Reine Mathilde in French), but historians no longer believe that; it seems to have been commissioned by William's half-brother Odo, Bishop of Bayeux, and made by Saxons in Kent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda bore William eleven children, and he was believed to have been faithful to her, at least up until the time their son Robert rebelled against his father and Matilda sided with Robert against William. After she died, in 1083
1 CONC  at the age of 51, William became tyrannical, and people blamed it on his having lost her. Contrary to the belief that she was buried at St. Stephen's, also called l'Abbaye-aux-Hommes inCaen, Normandy, where William was eventually
1 CONC  buried, she is intombed at l'Abbaye aux Dames, which is the Sainte-Trinité church, also in Caen. Of particular interest is the 11th century slab, a sleek black stone decorated with her epitaph, marking her grave at the rear of th
1 CONC e church. It is of special note since the grave marker for William was replaced as recently as the beginning of the 19th century. Years later, their graves were opened and their bones measured, proving their physical statures.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT Some doubt exists over how many daughters there were. This list includes some entries which are obscure.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Curthose (c. 1054 ? 1134), Duke of Normandy, married Sybil of Conversano, daughter of Geoffrey of Conversano
1 CONT Adeliza (or Alice) (c. 1055 ? ?), reportedly betrothed to Harold II of England (Her existence is in some doubt.)
1 CONT Cecilia (or Cecily) (c. 1056 ? 1126), Abbess of Holy Trinity, Caen
1 CONT William Rufus (1056 ? 1100), King of the English
1 CONT Richard (1057 ? c. 1081), killed by a stag in New Forest
1 CONT Adela (c. 1062 ? 1138), married Stephen, Count of Blois
1 CONT Agatha(c. 1064 ? c. 1080), betrothed to (1) Harold of Wessex, (2) Alfonso VI of Castile
1 CONT Constance (c. 1066 ? 1090), married Alan IV Fergent, Duke of Brittany; poisoned, possibly by her own servants
1 CONT Matilda (very obscure, her existence is in some doubt)
1 CONT Henry Beauclerc (1068?1135), King of England, married (1) Edith of Scotland, daughter of Malcolm III, King of Scotland, (2) Adeliza of Louvain
1 CONT Gundred (c. 1063 ? 1085), wife of William de Warenne (c. 1055 ? 1088), was formerly thought of as being yet another of Matilda's daughters, with speculation that she was William I's full daughter, a stepdaughter, or even a foundli
1 CONC ng or adopted daughter. However, this connection to William I has now been firmly debunked--see Gundred's discussionpage for further information.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda was a seventh generation direct descendent of Alfred the Great. Her marriage to William strengthened his claim to the throne through her. Every sovereign of England is directly descended continuously from her, including Qu
1 CONC een Elizabeth II.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Her love for her husband is referenced in the Award-winning play, Angels in America.
1 CONT
1 CONT ^ Hilliam, Paul (2005). William the Conqueror: First Norman King of England. New York City, New York: Rosen Publishing Group, 20. ISBN 1-4042-0166-1.
