1 CONC rs held by Brihtric in the time of King Edward are recorded as the possessions of Queen Matilda, and the remainder passed to Fitz Hamon.
1 CONT
1 CONT After her hand had been rejected by the noble Saxon, it is presumed she became the wife of a Fleming, named Gherbod, who appears to have held the hereditary office of Advocate of the Abbey of Saint Bertin, in St. Omers, and by who
1 CONC m she had at least two children, viz., Gherbod, to whom William gave the earldom of Chester, and Gundred, "the sister of Gherbod," and wife of William de Warren. Was this a clandestine or an informal marriage, which, as it has nev
1 CONC er been acknowledged by any chronicler, contemporary or other, might have been unknown to the Duke of Normandy, when he proposed to one whom he believed to be the maiden daughter of the Count of Flanders, and the corporal chastise
1 CONC ment inflicted, however unworthy of a man, passed over, sub silentio, for prudential reasons, by the parties who had been guilty of a disgraceful suppression of facts? The subsequent marriage under such circumstances will awaken n
1 CONC o surprise in any one who has studied the character of William. Utterly unscrupulous, destitute of every generous, noble, or delicate feeling, every action of his life was dictated by POLICY alone. An alliance with the Count of Fl
1 CONC anders might be considered by the crafty schemer sufficiently advantageous to warrant his overlooking any objectionable antecedents in the conduct of a granddaughter of a king of France, his first discovery of which had provoked h
1 CONC is savage nature into a momentary ebullition of fury. Her being the mother of two children was a point in her favour with a man whose sole motive for marrying was the perpetuation of a dynasty, and the fair prospect of legitimate 
1 CONC issue, in whose veins the blood of the Capets should enrich that of the Furrier of Falaise, would overcome any hesitation at espousing the widow of an Advocate of St. Bertin. On the other hand, Count Baldwin would be too happy to 
1 CONC embrace the opportunity of reinstating his daughter in a position befitting her birth, and, as well as the lady herself, gladly condone past insults for future advantages and the hope of smothering, in the splendour of a ducal wed
1 CONC ding, the awkward whispers of scandal.
1 CONT
1 CONT I have said thus much simply to show the view that may be taken of these mysterious circumstances, in opposition to the rose-coloured representations of some modern historians, who, upon no stronger evidence, elevate the Conqueror
1 CONC  into a model husband, and describe Matilda as the perfection of womankind.
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Weis' "Ancestral Roots. . ." (121:23), (162:23), (169:23).
1 CONT An interesting story is told in Cokayne's "Complete Peerage"(Gloucester). According to the Tewkesbury Abbey Chronicle, Brictric, son of Algar, otherwise known as Brictric Mawr, a great thegn of the time of Edward the Confessor, he
1 CONC ld, with Tewkesbury, various lordships in Worcester, Gloucester, Somerset, and other counties. Maud [Matilda], afterwards wife of WILLIAM THE CONQUERER, in her youth wished to marry Brictric, who refused her. When she became Queen
1 CONC , she imprisoned him, and on his death shortly afterwards his manors, the (later) honour of Gloucester, were given to her.
0 @N6000000003177913221@ NOTE QUEEN CONSORT 1066-1083. BURIED AT THE ABBEY OF LA 'TRINITE AT CAEN.
0 @N6000000003219753071@ NOTE Matilda of Flanders (c. 1031 – 2 November 1083) was Queen consort of the Kingdom of England and the wife of William I the Conqueror. Her love for her husband is referenced in the Award-winning play, Angels in America.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the daughter of count Baldwin V of Flanders, and Adèle (1000-1078/9), daughter of Robert II of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT At 4'2" tall, Matilda was Britain's smallest adult queen, according to the Guinness Book of Records. According to legend, Matilda (or "Maud") told the representative of William, Duke of Normandy (later king of England as William t
1 CONC he Conqueror), who had come asking for her hand, that she was far too high-born (being descended from King Alfred the Great of England) to consider marrying a bastard. When that was repeated to him, William, rode from Normandy to 
1 CONC Bruges, found Matilda on her way to church, dragged her off her horse by her long braids, threw her down in the street in front of her flabbergasted attendants, and then rode off. Another version of the story states that William r
1 CONC ode to Matilda's father's house in Lille, threw her to the ground in her room (again, by the braids), and hit her (or violently shook her) before leaving. Naturally Baldwin took offense at this but, before they drew swords, Matild
1 CONC a settled the matter. [1] by deciding to marry him, and even a papal ban (on the grounds of consanguinity) did not dissuade her. They were married in 1053.
1 CONT
1 CONT There were rumours that Matilda had been in love with the English ambassador to Flanders, a Saxon named Brihtric, who declined her advances. Whatever the truth of the matter, years later when she was acting as Regent for William i
1 CONC n England, she used her authority to confiscate Brihtric's lands and throw him into prison, where he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT When William was preparing to invade England, Matilda outfitted a ship, the Mora, out of her own money and gave it to him. For many years it was thought that she had something to do with creating the Bayeux Tapestry, but historian
1 CONC s no longer believe that; it seems to have been commissioned by William's half-brother Odo, Bishop of Bayeux, and made by Saxons in Kent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda bore William eleven children, and he was believed to have been faithful to her, at least up until the time their son Robert rebelled against his father and Matilda sided with Robert against William. After she died, in 1083
1 CONC  at the age of 51, William became tyrannical, and people blamed it on his having lost her. Contrary to the belief that she was buried at St. Stephen's, also called l'Abbaye-aux-Hommes in Caen, Normandy, where William was eventuall
1 CONC y buried, she is intombed at l'Abbaye aux Dames, which is the Sainte-Trinité church, also in Caen. Of particular interest is the 11th century slab, a sleek black stone decorated with her epitaph, marking her grave at the rear of t
1 CONC he church. It is of special note since the grave marker for William was replaced as recently as the beginning of the 19th century. Years later, their graves were opened and their bones measured, proving their physical statures.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Some doubt exists over how many daughters there were. This list includes some entries which are obscure.
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Robert Curthose (c. 1054 – 1134), Duke of Normandy, married Sybil of Conversano, daughter of Geoffrey of Conversano
1 CONT    2. Adeliza (or Alice) (c. 1055 – ?), reportedly betrothed to Harold II of England (Her existence is in some doubt.)
1 CONT    3. Cecilia (or Cecily) (c. 1056 – 1126), Abbess of Holy Trinity, Caen
1 CONT    4. William Rufus (1056 – 1100), King of the English
1 CONT    5. Richard (1057 – c. 1081), killed by a stag in New Forest
1 CONT    6. Adela (c. 1062 – 1138), married Stephen, Count of Blois
1 CONT    7. Agatha (c. 1064 – c. 1080), betrothed to (1) Harold of Wessex, (2) Alfonso VI of Castile
1 CONT    8. Constance (c. 1066 – 1090), married Alan IV Fergent, Duke of Brittany; poisoned, possibly by her own servants
1 CONT    9. Matilda (very obscure, her existence is in some doubt)
1 CONT   10. Henry Beauclerc (1068–1135), King of England, married (1) Edith of Scotland, daughter of Malcolm III, King of Scotland, (2) Adeliza of Louvain
1 CONT
1 CONT Gundred (c. 1063 – 1085), wife of William de Warenne (c. 1055 – 1088), was formerly thought of as being yet another of Matilda's daughters, with speculation that she was William I's full daughter, a step-daughter, or even a foundl
1 CONC ing or adopted daughter. However, this connection to William I has now been firmly debunked--see Gundred's discussion page for further information.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Matilda was a seventh generation direct descendent of Alfred the Great. Her marriage to William strengthened his claim to the throne through her. Every sovereign of England is directly descended continously from her, includi
1 CONC ng Queen Elizabeth II.
