0 @N6000000002187696252@ NOTE Bernard II, Duke of Saxony
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Bernard II (c. 990 – 29 June 1059) was the duke of Saxony (1011 – 1059), the third of the Billung dynasty, a son of Bernard I and Hildegard. He had the rights of a count in Frisia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Like his father, Bernard expanded the powers of the duke in Saxony and is regarded as the greatest of the Billungers. He was originally a supporter of Emperor Henry II and he accompanied him into Poland and negotiated the treaty o
1 CONC f Bautzen of 1018. In 1019-1020, he revolted and gained the recognition of the tribal laws of Saxony, something his father had just failed to do. He returned then to war with the Slavs and drew them into his sphere of power and in
1 CONC fluence through their leader, Godescalc (Gottschalk).
1 CONT
1 CONT He supported Conrad II in 1024 and his son Henry III, though he began to fear the latter for his closeness to the Archbishop Adalbert of Bremen, an inveterate enemy of the dukes of Saxony. Though he was a critical ally of the Dane
1 CONC s, so fundamental to Henry's wars in the Netherlands, Bernard was on the brink of rebellion until the death of Adalbert. The remainder of his reign, however, was quiet.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1045, he erected the Alsterburg in Hamburg. He died in 1059 and was succeeded without incident by his son Ordulf. He is buried in the Church of Saint Michael in Lüneburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bernard I Duke of Saxony
1 CONT 1011 – 1059 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ordulf 
